"""Authored single-best-answer study items; slide numbers are one-based."""
import json,random,pathlib
lectures=json.load(open('app/data/lectures.json'));bank=[]
def add(doc,rows):
 for line in rows.strip().split('\n'):
  if not line.strip():continue
  p,stem,correct,d1,d2,d3,explanation=line.split('|');page=int(p);options=[correct,d1,d2,d3];random.Random(len(bank)+19).shuffle(options)
  bank.append({'id':f'q{len(bank)+1:03}','lecture':doc,'page':page,'stem':stem,'options':options,'answer':options.index(correct),'explanation':explanation,'difficulty':'Application' if any(w in stem.lower() for w in ['patient','technician','observ','calculate','film shows','following','12 v','resistors']) else 'Recall'})
add(0,'''3|Which bone is the longest and strongest in the human body?|Femur|Tibia|Fibula|Humerus|The femur is the major bone of the thigh and is described as the longest and strongest bone. The tibia bears weight in the leg, while the fibula is more slender and the humerus belongs to the upper limb.
3|Which feature belongs to the proximal end of the femur?|Lesser trochanter|Medial malleolus|Tibial plateau|Patellar surface|The proximal femur contains the head, neck and greater and lesser trochanters. The patellar surface is distal; the tibial plateau and medial malleolus belong to the tibia.
4|Which proximal tibial surface articulates with the femoral condyles?|Tibial plateau|Tibial tuberosity|Medial malleolus|Anterior crest|The tibial plateau receives the femoral condyles at the knee. The tuberosity provides ligament attachment rather than the main femorotibial articular surface.
4|The patellar ligament attaches distally to which landmark?|Tibial tuberosity|Lesser trochanter|Fibular head|Medial femoral epicondyle|The roughened tibial tuberosity is the distal attachment of the patellar ligament. This links the patella to the tibia as part of the knee extensor mechanism.
5|Which of these is a tarsal bone?|Navicular|Scaphoid|Capitate|Lunate|The navicular is one of the tarsals in the foot. Scaphoid, capitate and lunate are carpal bones in the wrist.
6|Which vessels connect superficial and deep venous systems of the lower limb?|Perforating veins|Coronary arteries|Pulmonary veins|Carotid arteries|Lower-limb venous drainage includes superficial, deep and perforating veins. Perforating veins provide connections between the superficial and deep systems; the other options are not lower-limb connecting veins.
10|Which ligaments convert the sciatic notches into foramina?|Sacrotuberous and sacrospinous|Iliofemoral and pubofemoral|Patellar and collateral|Annular and cruciate|The sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments complete the boundaries of the greater and lesser sciatic foramina. These openings transmit structures between the pelvis and adjacent regions.
11|Which is a principal action of gluteus maximus?|Extension of the thigh|Extension of the toes|Dorsiflexion of the foot|Flexion of the wrist|Gluteus maximus extends and laterally rotates the thigh at the hip. Toe extension and ankle dorsiflexion involve leg muscles rather than gluteus maximus.
11|Which muscle belongs to the deep gluteal group?|Piriformis|Rectus femoris|Sartorius|Gracilis|Piriformis is listed among the small deep gluteal muscles. Rectus femoris and sartorius are anterior thigh muscles; gracilis is in the medial compartment.
12|The superior and inferior gluteal arteries arise from which artery?|Internal iliac artery|External carotid artery|Brachial artery|Popliteal artery|The major gluteal arterial branches arise from the internal iliac artery. Their pelvic origin explains their passage through the greater sciatic foramen to reach the gluteal region.
13|The hip is classified as which type of joint?|Ball-and-socket synovial|Pivot synovial|Fibrous suture|Primary cartilaginous|The spherical femoral head articulates with the acetabulum, allowing multiaxial movement. This arrangement defines a ball-and-socket synovial joint.
13|Which structure deepens the acetabular socket?|Acetabular labrum|Patellar ligament|Meniscus of the knee|Interosseous membrane|The acetabular labrum is a rim that increases socket depth and helps retain the femoral head. The other structures do not form the acetabular rim.
17|Which bone is a sesamoid within the quadriceps tendon?|Patella|Talus|Fibula|Navicular|The patella develops within the quadriceps tendon and articulates with the anterior distal femur. Its position is characteristic of a sesamoid bone.
18|Which bone fits into the ankle mortise?|Talus|Calcaneus|Cuboid|Navicular|The trochlea of the talus fits into the mortise formed by the distal tibia and fibula. The calcaneus lies below the talus and is not the bone received by the mortise.
20|In which position is the ankle mortise grip on the talus strongest?|Dorsiflexion|Plantarflexion|Toe flexion|Hip extension|Dorsiflexion brings the wider anterior trochlea into the mortise and tightens the malleolar grip. Plantarflexion brings the narrower portion into the mortise and is relatively less stable.
22|Which nerve primarily supplies the anterior thigh compartment?|Femoral nerve|Obturator nerve|Tibial nerve|Common fibular nerve|The anterior thigh compartment includes quadriceps and is primarily supplied by the femoral nerve. The obturator nerve mainly supplies medial thigh adductors.
23|Which nerve primarily supplies the medial thigh compartment?|Obturator nerve|Axillary nerve|Radial nerve|Deep fibular nerve|The medial compartment contains the adductors and is primarily supplied by the obturator nerve. Part of adductor magnus has additional sciatic innervation, so “primarily” is important.
24|Which muscle forms the lateral boundary of the femoral triangle?|Sartorius|Adductor longus|Gracilis|Biceps femoris|Sartorius forms the lateral boundary, adductor longus the medial boundary and the inguinal ligament the superior boundary of the femoral triangle.
26|Where is the popliteal fossa located?|Behind the knee|In front of the ankle|Above the inguinal ligament|At the medial wrist|The popliteal fossa is the diamond-shaped depression posterior to the knee. It transmits important nerves and vessels between the thigh and leg.
28|Which pair forms the hindfoot?|Talus and calcaneus|Navicular and cuboid|First and second metatarsals|Medial and lateral cuneiforms|The hindfoot contains the talus and calcaneus. Navicular, cuboid and the cuneiforms form the midfoot; metatarsals form part of the forefoot.
30|Which ligament helps support the medial longitudinal arch?|Spring ligament|Annular ligament|Coracoacromial ligament|Patellar ligament|The spring ligament helps support the medial longitudinal arch together with tibialis posterior and the plantar aponeurosis. The other named ligaments are not supports of this arch.''')
add(1,'''4|Which substance in radiographic film is responsible for photosensitivity?|Silver halide|Polyester|Lead foil|Cellulose backing alone|The emulsion contains photosensitive silver-halide crystals. Polyester supports the film and lead foil reduces backscatter in dental packets; neither is the photosensitive constituent.
5|Which film arrangement is typically used for high-detail film-screen mammography?|Single emulsion|No emulsion|Four separate emulsion bases|Opaque metallic base|Single-emulsion film is selected where fine detail is important and is used in mammography film-screen systems. The lecture contrasts this with double-emulsion film used commonly in general screen-film radiography.
9|What is the main function of the radiographic film base?|Mechanical support|Formation of metallic silver|Removal of unexposed crystals|Conversion of X-rays into visible light|The polyester base supports the emulsion and provides strength and rigidity. Image formation occurs in the emulsion, while intensifying screens convert X-rays to light.
9|Why is a modern polyester film base commonly tinted blue?|To reduce viewing eye strain|To make the film radioactive|To replace the fixer|To increase chemical fog|The blue tint improves viewing comfort on a viewbox. It is not the photosensitive element and cannot replace chemical processing.
10|Which film layer forms the image?|Emulsion|Polyester base|Adhesive alone|Outer packaging|The emulsion holds silver-halide crystals in gelatin and is the image-forming layer. The base and adhesive provide support and attachment.
10|What is the role of gelatin in the emulsion?|Holds crystals and permits chemical penetration|Blocks all processing chemicals|Acts as the X-ray generator|Removes all metallic silver|Gelatin distributes and holds the silver-halide crystals while allowing developer and fixer to reach them. Blocking processing chemicals would prevent development.
11|Which silver halide is commonly the main constituent of radiographic film crystals?|Silver bromide|Silver nitrate alone|Silver sulfate|Silver acetate|Silver bromide is the main halide described, sometimes with smaller quantities of iodide or chloride. These crystals undergo the exposure changes that create the latent image.
12|What is the latent image?|Invisible exposure-induced change|Fully dried radiograph|Fixer precipitate|Visible lead marker|The latent image is the invisible change in exposed silver-halide crystals. Development converts those changes into visible black metallic silver.
13|What is the main purpose of an intensifying screen?|Convert X-rays to visible light|Remove unexposed halide|Generate tube voltage|Dry the film|An intensifying screen absorbs X-rays and emits light that exposes compatible film efficiently. Fixing and drying are separate processing functions.
13|A technician observes blur from poor screen-film contact. What should be checked?|Cassette and screen contact|Only the wash-water drain|Only the report printer|Only the room clock|Poor contact allows emitted screen light to spread before reaching the film, reducing sharpness. Cassette condition and contact therefore form part of quality assurance.
14|Which layer bonds the emulsion to the film base?|Adhesive or subbing layer|Supercoat|Lead foil|Scintillator|The adhesive, substratum or subbing layer prevents the emulsion from lifting off the base during handling and processing. The supercoat protects the outer surface.
15|What is the principal function of the supercoat?|Protection from scratches and pressure|Dissolving unexposed silver halide|Storing digital pixel values|Generating X-rays|The thin hard gelatin supercoat protects the emulsion from mechanical damage. It is distinct from the photosensitive emulsion and from processing chemicals.
20|What is the purpose of lead foil backing in intraoral dental film packets?|Reduce backscatter reaching film|Increase film humidity|Replace the emulsion|Develop the latent image|Lead foil backing reduces radiation backscatter that could reach the film and degrade the image. The packet must also remain light-tight and protected from moisture.
24|Developer primarily converts exposed silver-halide crystals into what?|Black metallic silver|Clear polyester|Lead oxide|Water-soluble gelatin|Developer reduces exposed crystals to metallic silver, creating the visible image. Fixer instead removes unexposed silver halide.
25|What happens if washing after fixing is inadequate?|Residual chemicals may cause later staining|All metallic silver immediately disappears|The base becomes a digital detector|The film becomes immune to light|Residual fixer and related compounds impair archival stability and may cause discoloration. Washing removes these processing residues before drying and filing.
26|How should unprocessed radiographic film be handled?|By the edges with clean dry hands|By gripping the emulsion with wet hands|By sharply folding the centre|By dragging it over rough surfaces|Edge handling reduces fingerprints, pressure marks and scratches. Wet or contaminated fingers can produce chemical artefacts before the film even enters processing.
27|Which stock-control method best reduces expired film use?|First-expiry-first-out|Newest film always first|Random box selection|Ignore expiry dates|Using the earliest-expiring stock first reduces age-related waste and fog. Batch numbers and expiry dates should remain traceable.
29|A film shows black branching tree-like marks. What is the likely cause?|Static discharge|Excessive washing|Correct fixation|Normal screen contact|Static discharge produces branching black marks, particularly with dry air and rapid film removal. Gentle handling and humidity control reduce this artefact.
33|Why should film spectral sensitivity match the intensifying screen?|For efficient response to emitted light|To eliminate all need for radiation|To replace cassette light-tightness|To prevent any need for fixing|Film must respond to the screen’s emitted wavelengths to form the image efficiently. A mismatch wastes useful light and may lead to unnecessary exposure.
35|Green-sensitive film is also called what?|Orthochromatic film|Panchromatic film only|Duplicating paper|Lead-backed film|Orthochromatic radiographic film is sensitive to green light and pairs with compatible green-emitting rare-earth screens. Safelight selection must follow the film specification.
41|Which adjustment changes the displayed tissue range on a CT hard copy?|Window settings|Cassette hinge tension|Developer tank lid color|Lead marker thickness|Window settings select the grey-scale display of CT attenuation values, allowing different presentations for lung, bone or soft tissue. Physical film handling cannot perform that numerical display adjustment.''')
add(2,'''8|Which computer component temporarily holds active programs and data?|RAM|SSD|Printer|Barcode scanner|Random-access memory holds data in current use. An SSD provides longer-term storage; printers are output devices and barcode scanners are input devices.
8|Which component processes data and controls computer operations?|CPU|Monitor|Mouse|External hard disk|The central processing unit executes instructions and coordinates operations. The other devices provide input, output or storage.
8|Which is an input device used in radiography?|Barcode scanner|Diagnostic monitor|Film printer|Loudspeaker|A barcode scanner enters identification data into a system. Monitors, printers and speakers deliver output to a user.
8|Which device provides persistent image storage?|Solid-state drive|RAM alone|Mouse|Keyboard|An SSD retains stored data when power is off, unlike ordinary RAM. Secure backups are still needed because persistent storage can fail.
9|Which imaging system reconstructs cross-sectional images from multiple X-ray projections?|CT|PACS|RIS|Word processor|CT computer systems reconstruct tomographic images from multiple X-ray projections. PACS stores images, RIS manages workflow and word processors handle documents.
13|What is the main role of PACS?|Store retrieve and distribute medical images|Heat the X-ray tube filament|Develop silver-halide film|Generate mains electricity|PACS supports image archiving, retrieval, display and distribution. It is an information system rather than an X-ray production or chemical-processing device.
14|Which system primarily manages appointments worklists and radiology reports?|RIS|Scintillator|Fixer tank|Collimator|A Radiology Information System handles departmental workflow, patient registration, examination scheduling and reporting. PACS focuses on images rather than these administrative workflows.
17|What does DICOM provide?|A standard for medical image exchange|A developer chemical|A lead shielding thickness|An exposure timer circuit|Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine standardizes medical image formatting and exchange. It is a communication standard, not a physical detector or chemical.
18|Which is an example of raw technical data?|Recorded kVp value|Final interpreted diagnostic report|Signed clinical conclusion|Narrative discharge summary|A recorded kVp value is a raw technical fact. A diagnostic report is processed and interpreted information that supports clinical decisions.
19|What best describes information?|Data processed into meaningful results|Only unorganized detector counts|Only empty database fields|Only unread electrical signals|Information has been processed or interpreted to provide meaning. Raw readings and unorganized facts are data before that transformation.
24|A report reaches the clinician after the decision is already made. Which quality is most directly lacking?|Timeliness|Font variation|Screen size|Keyboard speed|Information must be available when needed to support decisions. Accuracy and completeness matter too, but the particular problem here is late delivery.
25|Which application is most suitable for preparing a departmental referral letter?|Word processor|Detector calibration tool|TFT readout controller|Exposure meter|Word processors create, edit and format text documents such as letters and reports. Detector tools and exposure meters serve different technical functions.
28|Which feature produces personalized letters from a template and data source?|Mail merge|Window leveling|Laser scanning|Disk defragmentation|Mail merge combines a document template with records to create individualized documents. Image windowing and laser scanning do not perform document personalization.
31|Which application best supports calculations and departmental statistics?|Spreadsheet|Presentation viewer|Image receptor|Audio recorder|Spreadsheet software organizes numeric data and supports calculations and statistical summaries. Word processors and presentation tools are better suited to prose and slides.
33|Medical X-ray CT and MRI images are commonly exchanged in which format?|DICOM|MP3|Plain TXT only|Executable EXE|DICOM can retain image data and associated medical information in a standardized form. Audio files and executables are not standard medical image exchange formats.
35|What is the purpose of file backup?|Provide a recoverable additional copy|Guarantee no access control is needed|Permanently erase the original|Increase radiation output|A backup protects against loss or damage to the primary file. It must itself be secured and does not replace access controls.
41|Which practice best protects transmitted patient images?|Use approved encrypted systems|Post identifiable images publicly|Share a single public password|Use any unapproved messaging service|Approved encrypted channels and authorized access protect confidentiality. Public sharing and shared credentials expose identifiable clinical data.
41|What should a technician verify before transmitting images?|Correct patient identifiers|The desktop wallpaper|The keyboard manufacturer|The monitor stand color|Correct identifiers help ensure images are associated with the intended patient and recipient workflow. Transmission speed does not compensate for a patient-identification error.
11|What is a clinical risk of excessive digital image processing?|Important findings may be obscured|X-ray exposure is always eliminated|All source data become more accurate|Radiation dose is retrospectively removed|Overprocessing may introduce misleading appearances or hide detail. Processing changes image presentation and cannot undo radiation already delivered.
39|What is a major benefit of teleradiology?|Access to remote specialist interpretation|Elimination of patient identity checks|Replacement of all radiographers|Removal of all network requirements|Digital communication allows images to reach specialists at other locations. It still requires secure systems, correct identifiers and appropriate professional roles.''')
add(3,'''5|What makes daylight film handling possible?|A light-tight enclosed handling system|Film becoming insensitive before development|Removing all silver halide before exposure|Unlimited exposure to room light|The enclosure shields sensitive film from ambient light during handling. Daylight processing never means that unprocessed film can be left uncovered in ordinary room light.
6|What occurs after the cassette is placed into a daylight loading compartment?|Protected cassette opening and film extraction|Immediate public display of unprocessed film|Removal of the X-ray tube anode|Conversion of the room into a laser scanner|The cassette opens inside a protected compartment and the exposed film is extracted safely. It then passes through chemical-processing stages.
7|Which component prevents ambient-light fogging in a daylight processor?|Light-tight chamber|Collection tray alone|Open wash basin|Uncovered feed slot|The light-tight chamber prevents room light from reaching sensitive film. The collection tray receives finished film but is not the main protection during loading.
9|Which limitation remains with daylight film processing?|Chemical maintenance is still required|No mechanical component can fail|Film is insensitive to all light|All running costs disappear|Daylight systems improve handling convenience but still rely on chemical processing and mechanical equipment. Chemical replenishment and maintenance remain necessary.
11|How does a daylight loader differ from a film processor?|Loader protects and transfers film; processor performs chemical processing|Both are always the same device|Loader alone converts X-rays to digital pixels|Processor only stores cassettes|The lecture distinguishes light-protected film transfer from chemical development, fixing, washing and drying. Some installations integrate both functions, but they remain distinct tasks.
13|Which receptor is used in computed radiography?|Photostimulable phosphor plate|Ordinary photographic paper|Direct-conversion selenium panel only|Bare lead sheet|CR stores a latent image in a photostimulable phosphor plate and requires reader scanning. Direct flat-panel detectors are a different receptor system.
14|What is the function of an ADC?|Convert analog signals to numerical values|Convert digital values into fixer|Generate the patient request form|Shield the operator from scatter|An analog-to-digital converter samples and quantizes an electrical signal into digital numerical values. It does not perform shielding or chemical processing.
18|How does a CR plate store a latent image?|Energy trapped in phosphor electron states|A wet metallic-silver film surface|A permanently heated tungsten filament|Printed text on the cassette|X-ray energy traps electrons in metastable states within the phosphor. Laser stimulation later releases stored energy as light for readout.
19|What releases stored image energy during CR readout?|Laser stimulation|Fixer immersion|Manual film drying|Mains switch removal|The CR reader scans the plate with a laser, stimulating emission of light from trapped energy. That emitted light is then detected and converted to an electrical signal.
20|Why is a CR plate exposed to intense light after readout?|Erase residual trapped energy|Add an extra patient exposure|Coat it with metallic silver|Create a permanent paper copy|Erasure removes residual stored energy and prepares the plate for reuse. Incomplete erasure can leave residual-image artefacts.
21|What physical principle underlies CR plate readout?|Photostimulable luminescence|Chemical precipitation alone|Thermionic emission from the patient|Magnetic induction in gelatin|CR phosphor releases stored energy as light when stimulated by a laser. This is photostimulable luminescence and differs from film chemical development.
22|Which CR plate layer stores X-ray exposure energy?|Photostimulable phosphor layer|Protective housing alone|Support base alone|Cassette latch|The phosphor layer holds the latent image. Protective and support layers provide mechanical protection but do not perform the principal storage function.
28|Which material is associated with direct-conversion DR?|Amorphous selenium|Silver bromide film|Barium sulfate contrast|Polyester alone|Amorphous selenium converts X-ray energy directly into electrical charge. Indirect detectors first convert X-rays into light through a scintillator.
31|Which material can act as a scintillator in indirect DR?|Cesium iodide|Sodium thiosulfate|Acetic acid|Polyester film base|Cesium iodide converts X-rays into visible light. Photodiodes then convert this light into charge; thiosulfate and acetic acid belong to film-processing chemistry.
33|What converts scintillator light to charge in indirect DR?|Photodiode|Fixer|Lead marker|Wash tank|The photodiode converts light into electrical charge. The scintillator performs the preceding X-ray-to-light conversion.
34|What is the role of the TFT array?|Switch and read out pixel charge|Dissolve unexposed crystals|Accelerate tube electrons|Provide patient immobilisation|Thin-film transistors act as switches that allow pixel signals to be read by detector electronics. They are part of detector readout rather than X-ray production.
39|Which workflow difference distinguishes CR from most DR systems?|CR requires a separate plate reader|DR always requires chemical development|CR never uses cassettes|DR cannot transmit images|CR stores a plate image that must be scanned in a reader. Most DR systems read electronic detector signals directly, improving image availability.
41|Why can acceptable digital brightness conceal excessive exposure?|Processing can normalize image appearance|Patient dose is erased by windowing|The detector blocks all radiation|Image brightness directly measures patient dose|Digital processing can maintain acceptable brightness over a wide exposure range. Exposure indicators and technique monitoring are therefore needed to reduce dose creep.
42|Which operation adjusts displayed contrast and brightness?|Windowing and leveling|Fixing and washing|Cassette closing|Developer replenishment|Windowing and leveling remap digital image values for display. They change presentation and do not alter the exposure that has already occurred.
42|Can processing reliably recover anatomy that was never captured?|No; acquisition must contain the information|Yes; rotation creates missing anatomy|Yes; cropping reconstructs any tissue|Yes; brightness adjustment replaces positioning|Postprocessing can improve presentation of acquired data. It cannot reliably recreate anatomy outside the captured field or information absent from the original acquisition.
43|Does switching to digital imaging automatically reduce patient dose?|No; exposure technique still requires optimisation|Yes in every case|Yes because detectors emit no light|Yes because PACS removes radiation|Digital imaging offers dose-optimisation opportunities but does not guarantee lower exposure. Technique, detector performance and radiation-protection practice remain essential.''')
add(4,'''5|Which electrode receives accelerated electrons in an X-ray tube?|Anode|Cathode|Collimator|Filter|The positive anode receives electrons emitted from the negative cathode. Their interaction with the target generates heat and X-rays.
5|What is the function of the cathode assembly?|Emit and focus electrons|Develop radiographic film|Absorb all scattered radiation|Store patient records|The cathode provides electron emission from the heated filament and helps focus electrons toward the target. The anode receives those electrons.
5|Why is filtration placed in the X-ray beam?|Remove low-energy photons with little imaging benefit|Increase unnecessary skin dose|Replace all beam collimation|Convert film into a digital image|Low-energy photons may be absorbed in the patient without contributing useful detector information. Filtration reduces that low-energy component; collimation separately limits field size.
6|What is the primary purpose of high tube voltage?|Accelerate electrons toward the anode|Dry film emulsion|Open the cassette latch|Store the image in PACS|A large potential difference provides electrons with kinetic energy as they cross the evacuated tube. Filament heating uses a different low-voltage high-current circuit.
16|What does an exposure timer control?|Duration of X-ray production|Color of the room light|Fixer pH|Image file naming|The timer terminates X-ray production after the selected exposure interval. Timing affects delivered exposure and must be reproducible.
17|Which is a function of the X-ray generator?|Control tube voltage current and exposure time|Store all radiology reports|Replace all beam-limiting devices|Provide chemical fixation|The generator supplies and controls electrical conditions for X-ray production. Patient records and film-processing chemistry are separate systems.
18|Which device restricts the X-ray field to the area of interest?|Collimator|Densitometer|Film dryer|PACS server|Collimators limit the exposed area and reduce unnecessary irradiation and scatter production. They act before radiation enters the patient.
18|Which is a beam-centering device?|Light-beam localizer|Fixer tank|Silver recovery cartridge|Film hopper|A light-beam localizer indicates the beam field and assists alignment with the anatomical area. It does not chemically process or store film.
19|What is the safety role of an anode rotation interlock?|Prevent exposure when rotation is inadequate|Increase exposure regardless of tube readiness|Bypass all circuit protection|Switch off the wash-water supply|An interlock prevents unsafe exposure when required operating conditions are not met. Inadequate anode rotation may concentrate heat and damage the target.
19|What is the principal function of a fuse?|Interrupt excessive current|Store electric charge indefinitely|Increase image contrast directly|Rotate the detector|A fuse breaks the circuit when current exceeds its safe capacity. It protects electrical equipment rather than directly changing detector image contrast.
21|What initiates electron emission in a conventional X-ray tube?|Heating the cathode filament|Cooling the anode to zero kelvin|Opening the collimator mirror|Immersing the tube in fixer|Heating the tungsten filament releases electrons by thermionic emission. High voltage then accelerates these electrons toward the target.
22|Bremsstrahlung results primarily from what?|Electron deceleration near a target nucleus|Fixer removing unexposed crystals|Laser erasure of a CR plate|Window-level adjustment|The incoming electron loses kinetic energy as it is deflected and decelerated in the nuclear electric field. The lost energy is emitted as an X-ray photon, producing a continuous spectrum.
22|What event leads to characteristic radiation?|An outer electron fills an inner-shell vacancy|Film is exposed to a safelight|A laser erases residual plate energy|A grid moves across the detector|An incident electron ejects an inner-shell electron. When another electron fills that vacancy, the energy difference is emitted as a characteristic photon.
23|What is a main effect of scatter reaching the receptor?|Reduced image contrast|Guaranteed improvement in detail|Elimination of all patient exposure|Conversion of X-rays to magnetic fields|Scatter adds unwanted exposure that is not correctly related to the primary anatomical path. This degrades contrast and may add noise.
24|Where is an anti-scatter grid placed?|Between patient and image receptor|Between the filament and focusing cup|Inside the developer tank|Behind the reporting monitor|A grid intercepts scatter emerging from the patient before it reaches the image receptor while allowing appropriately aligned primary radiation through.
24|How is grid ratio defined?|Lead strip height divided by interspace width|Film width divided by film length|Exposure time divided by tube current|Source distance divided by patient age|Grid ratio compares lead-strip height with the distance between strips. It describes grid geometry and influences scatter rejection and alignment demands.
25|Why is a grid moved during exposure in a Bucky assembly?|Blur grid-line shadows|Generate thermionic emission|Remove all patient motion|Change the atomic number of lead|Grid movement spreads strip shadows over the exposure so distinct grid lines are less visible. It does not immobilize the patient or produce tube electrons.
26|What is an important trade-off when using an anti-scatter grid?|Exposure factors and patient dose may increase|Patient dose is always zero|Tube loading always disappears|No alignment is required|A grid absorbs some primary radiation as well as scatter. Exposure compensation may therefore be needed, increasing patient dose; grid use should be justified for the examination.
28|Which modality provides real-time moving X-ray images?|Fluoroscopy|Plain film storage|Word processing|Densitometry|Fluoroscopy produces real-time images of internal movement. Film storage and densitometry do not perform real-time patient imaging.
28|Which equipment is commonly used for intraoperative real-time imaging?|C-arm|Darkroom hopper|Film drying cabinet|Word-processing terminal|The mobile C-arm positions an X-ray source and receptor around the operative field for procedural imaging. The other devices serve processing or administrative functions.''')
add(5,'''2|Which mechanism advances film through an automatic processor?|Roller transport system|PACS router|Lead grid|Exposure switch|Roller racks and crossover components guide film through solutions and the dryer at controlled speed. This mechanical consistency helps standardize processing time.
3|Which subsystem helps keep chemical baths homogeneous?|Recirculation and filtration|Image cropping|Patient registration|Collimator shutters|Recirculation mixes solutions, limits temperature gradients and filters particles. These actions support consistent chemical processing across the film surface.
4|What is the purpose of automatic replenishment?|Maintain chemical activity and solution levels|Replace all washing|Make film insensitive before exposure|Remove the need for quality control|Processing consumes and dilutes chemistry, while oxidation also reduces activity. Replenishment introduces fresh chemicals to stabilize performance, but does not replace QA.
6|How should films be fed to reduce roller jams?|Straight and appropriately spaced|Overlapped and folded|Crooked and tightly bunched|With wet fingers on the emulsion|Straight, spaced feeding reduces overlap and transport misalignment. Bent or overlapping films can jam and damage the emulsion.
6|What is the purpose of standby mode?|Conserve resources while idle|Expose films to room light|Disable every safety sensor|Remove the need for chemistry|Standby reduces activity when no film is being processed and resumes operation when film is detected. It is an efficiency function, not permission to bypass processing conditions.
8|Which variables are standardized in automatic processing?|Time temperature and chemical activity|Only wall color and room size|Only patient age|Only cassette label font|Automatic systems control transport time, bath temperature and chemistry to achieve reproducible processing. Each variable affects image density and contrast.
10|Why is developer temperature controlled precisely?|Chemical activity depends on temperature|Temperature only changes patient identity|It replaces all transport rollers|It guarantees no chemical oxidation|Developer reaction rate changes with temperature. Poor regulation can alter density, contrast and fog, so the actual temperature should be verified.
11|Which sequence describes the automatic processor path?|Developer then fixer then wash then dryer|Fixer then developer then dryer then wash|Dryer then wash then developer then fixer|Wash then dryer then fixer then developer|Automatic film processing develops the image, fixes it, removes chemical residues by washing and then dries it. Reordering these stages would not form a stable diagnostic film.
12|What commonly detects film entry at the feed tray?|Microswitch or optical sensor|Patient dosimeter|Collimator lamp|DICOM viewer|Entry sensors detect film and trigger transport, drying and replenishment functions. A dosimeter measures exposure rather than film entry.
13|Which feed arrangement permits handling under normal room light?|Light-tight daylight loader|Uncovered darkroom tray|Open film hanger|Bare workbench|The enclosed daylight loader prevents room light from reaching sensitive film. An ordinary uncovered tray still requires a darkroom and appropriate safelight conditions.
14|Which rollers first grip film just beyond the feed tray?|Entrance rollers|Squeegee rollers|Dryer exhaust fan|Water filter|Entrance rollers engage film at the start of transport. Deep-tank rollers guide it through chemistry and squeegee rollers remove excess water later.
15|What is the principal role of crossover rollers?|Guide film between adjacent processing tanks|Generate tube voltage|Erase a CR plate|Measure patient blood pressure|Crossover assemblies redirect film from one rack or tank to the next. Dirty or misaligned parts can cause marks and scratches.
15|What do squeegee rollers do before drying?|Remove excess surface water|Add more fixer to the emulsion|Expose film to X-rays|Generate a latent image|Squeegee rollers press excess water from the film and reduce the drying load. This helps the dryer produce a dry finished film efficiently.
16|Volume replenishment is commonly triggered according to what?|Film dimensions or area processed|Patient blood group|Room wallpaper|Report word count|Entry sensors estimate film dimensions or processed area and the system delivers a specified replenisher volume. This relates replenishment to workload.
17|Why is timed flood replenishment useful in low-volume departments?|Combat oxidation despite low film throughput|Replace the X-ray generator|Prevent every form of patient motion|Eliminate all solution ageing|Chemistry can oxidize even when few films are processed. Timed replenishment introduces fresh chemistry at intervals independent of film throughput.
17|Why should standard manual chemistry not simply be used in an automatic processor?|Automatic chemistry must tolerate high speed and temperature|Manual chemistry contains no water|Automatic films have no emulsion|Rollers remove all need for hardeners|Rapid processing at elevated temperatures requires specially formulated chemistry, including control of emulsion swelling. The manufacturer’s specified chemistry should be followed.
18|Which agent acts as a developer hardener in automatic processing?|Glutaraldehyde|Cesium iodide|Lead oxide|Barium sulfate|Glutaraldehyde limits gelatin swelling under automatic-processing conditions. Cesium iodide is a scintillator and barium sulfate is a contrast material.
20|Why is a cleaner film passed before patient films during startup?|Remove roller-path deposits and debris|Create a calibration patient exposure|Replace fresh fixer|Measure patient vital signs|A cleaner film lifts dried chemistry and debris from the transport pathway. This reduces contamination and artefacts on subsequent patient films.
21|A film shows repeating roller-related marks. What should be inspected?|Dirty or damaged rollers|Only the report template|Only the patient wristband|Only the cassette label|Contamination or damage on a rotating roller can produce recurring marks. Cleaning or replacing the affected component addresses the cause; mark spacing depends on roller geometry.
22|Which instrument measures control-film optical density?|Densitometer|Thermometer|Sphygmomanometer|Voltmeter|A densitometer measures optical density at control-film steps. These values help track base plus fog, speed and contrast as part of processor quality assurance.''')
add(6,'''5|What is a monobath?|One solution providing development and fixation|A separate water-only rinse|A digital detector calibration|A dry storage hopper|A monobath combines developing and fixing actions in one balanced solution. Washing and drying remain necessary after this combined chemical stage.
6|Which chemical provides the fixing action in a monobath?|Thiosulfate|Calcium tungstate|Cesium iodide|Polyester|Thiosulfate acts as a silver-halide solvent, dissolving unexposed crystals. The developer and fixer actions must be balanced in a monobath.
8|What is an important limitation of monobath processing?|Limited control of contrast and image quality|Unlimited solution life|No need for timing|No chemical exhaustion|Combining two chemical functions reduces independent control and the solution can exhaust rapidly. The lecture therefore does not recommend it for routine diagnostic work.
11|What distinguishes conventional manual processing?|Separate developer rinse fixer and wash stages|All chemistry in one unlabelled tank|No temperature control|No film hangers ever used|Separate baths allow each chemical stage to be controlled and maintained independently. A monobath instead combines development and fixation.
15|What is the usual chemical character of developer?|Alkaline|Strongly acidic fixer|Pure inert gas|Dry metallic powder|The developer is alkaline, which supports the reducing agents. Fixer is acidic and removes unexposed silver halide.
17|According to the lecture what is a typical manual development condition?|About 5 minutes at 20°C|About 5 seconds at 80°C|About 1 hour at 0°C|About 30 minutes at 60°C|The lecture gives approximately five minutes at 20°C as a reference manual condition. Actual time-temperature combinations should follow the film and chemical manufacturer’s chart.
18|Why should film move through tanks in one direction?|Reduce carry-back contamination|Make all tanks identical|Eliminate chemical ageing|Avoid using a timer|A one-way workflow prevents film and drips from contaminating stages it has already left. Separate lids and stirring rods also help preserve solution purity.
22|What is the function of the master tank water jacket?|Stabilize solution temperature|Store final reports|Produce scattered radiation|Replace every inner tank|Water surrounding the inner tanks exchanges heat through the walls and helps maintain a common stable temperature. It is not itself a substitute for developer or fixer.
26|Which darkroom layout principle reduces film contamination?|Separate wet and dry zones|Mix wet chemicals on the loading bench|Keep unexposed film beside open fixer|Use porous untreated floors|A dry zone supports clean film loading while a wet zone contains chemical processing. Separation reduces water, chemical and handling artefacts.
28|What is the primary use of the darkroom dry bench?|Cassette unloading and film loading|Running-water washing|Mixing fixer in open trays|Silver-sludge disposal|The dry bench provides a clean area for handling sensitive film and cassettes. Wet chemical work belongs at the wet bench.
29|What is a film hopper used for?|Light-protected storage of unexposed film|Heating the X-ray anode|Measuring optical density|Sterilizing needles|A hopper stores unexposed film ready for loading while protecting it from light. It is part of the dry-side storage system.
31|Which material is suitable for a processing tank?|Stainless steel 316|Copper|Brass|Galvanised steel|The lecture recommends stainless steel 316 or polypropylene for chemical resistance. Copper, brass and galvanized metals can corrode or contaminate processing chemistry.
33|Why should processing tanks be covered when idle?|Limit oxidation and contamination|Increase dust entry|Accelerate developer exhaustion|Expose film to white light|Covers reduce contact with air and keep out dust. This helps preserve chemistry and supports consistent image quality.
37|Which developer ingredient acts as a restrainer to limit fog?|Potassium bromide|Thiosulfate|Acetic acid|Alum|Potassium bromide restrains development of unexposed crystals and limits fog. Thiosulfate clears film in fixer, acetic acid acidifies and alum hardens gelatin.
37|What is the function of sulfite in developer?|Slow oxidation|Emit X-rays|Form the film base|Increase static discharge|Sulfite is a preservative that slows oxidation of developing agents. It helps maintain chemical activity during use and storage.
38|Which fixer ingredient dissolves unexposed silver halide?|Thiosulfate|Hydroquinone|Potassium carbonate|Polyester|Thiosulfate is the clearing agent in fixer. Hydroquinone is a developing agent and carbonate provides alkalinity in developer.
39|A film remains milky after fixation. What is a likely cause?|Exhausted or inadequate fixer|Correct complete fixation|Perfect archival washing|Excessively clean dry bench|Insufficient removal of unexposed halide leaves a cloudy or milky appearance. Fixer strength and clearing time should be checked.
43|A technician sees pale circular spots caused by bubbles during development. What is the prevention?|Agitate and tap the hanger|Skip all agitation|Add fixer to developer|Dry the film before fixing|Air bells prevent developer from contacting parts of the emulsion and produce pale spots. Initial agitation and tapping dislodge these bubbles.
43|Fixer splashed on film before development most likely causes what?|White spots|Uniform increased density|Black branching static lines|Sharper bone edges|Fixer removes silver-halide material before it can develop, producing local light or white spots. Developer splashes can instead cause dark spots.
48|What is the purpose of the rinse between developer and fixer?|Remove developer carryover and halt development|Create a second latent image|Add more exposed crystals|Replace final washing|Rinsing removes alkaline developer from the emulsion and reduces contamination of the acidic fixer. It protects fixer life and helps reduce staining.
49|The lecture recommends fixing for at least what relation to clearing time?|Twice the clearing time|One tenth of clearing time|Exactly zero seconds|Half the clearing time|Clearing time indicates removal of visible cloudiness, while additional fixing supports more complete stabilization. The lecture gives at least twice clearing time, with manufacturer guidance controlling practice.
50|What can cause brown staining during film storage?|Inadequate final washing|Correct archival washing|Clean filtered drying air|Correct stock rotation alone|Residual thiosulfate and silver compounds can cause later staining. Adequate running-water washing is necessary for archival stability.
54|Which used solution is the main source of dissolved silver for recovery?|Fixer|Fresh wash water before use|Unopened developer replenisher|Pure distilled water|Fixer dissolves unexposed silver halide and accumulates silver compounds. It is therefore the main liquid source for silver recovery.
55|Which silver recovery method deposits silver on a cathode?|Electrolytic recovery|Film drying|Safelight testing|Mechanical agitation only|Electrolytic recovery uses electrical current to deposit silver on a cathode. Metallic replacement instead uses a more reactive metal such as iron.
55|Steel wool cartridges recover silver by which process?|Metallic replacement|Thermionic emission|Photostimulable luminescence|Digital reconstruction|Iron displaces silver from solution in a metallic-replacement process. This is a chemical recovery method rather than an imaging or readout mechanism.
56|How should used fixer be managed?|Collect in labelled sealed containers for approved recovery or disposal|Pour directly down a drain|Mix into fresh developer|Leave unlabelled in a patient area|Used fixer contains silver and should be collected securely with disposal records. Recovery or approved waste handling prevents avoidable environmental contamination.''')
add(7,'''7|What is the SI unit of radioactive activity?|Becquerel|Gray|Sievert|Volt|The becquerel corresponds to one nuclear decay per second. Gray measures absorbed dose, sievert measures protection-weighted dose and volt measures potential difference.
7|What does radioactive half-life describe?|Time for half the radioactive atoms to decay|Time for all radiation to cease|Time for temperature to halve|Time for every atom to gain an electron|Half-life is the time required for half of the initial radioactive nuclei to decay. It does not imply the entire sample becomes nonradioactive after one half-life.
9|What is the SI unit of absorbed dose?|Gray|Becquerel|Ampere|Ohm|Absorbed dose measures energy deposited per unit mass. One gray is one joule per kilogram; activity and electrical quantities have different units.
9|Equivalent and effective doses are expressed in which SI unit?|Sievert|Gray per second only|Coulomb|Watt|The sievert is used for protection quantities that incorporate radiation weighting, and for effective dose also tissue weighting. Absorbed dose itself is measured in gray.
9|One gray corresponds to how many rad?|100 rad|1 rad|10 rad|1000 rad|The lecture gives 1 Gy = 100 rad. These are different units of absorbed dose, not units of activity.
9|One sievert corresponds to how many rem?|100 rem|1 rem|10 rem|1000 rem|The sievert and rem measure equivalent or effective dose, with 1 Sv = 100 rem. They should not be confused with gray and rad for absorbed dose.
12|What is the SI unit of force?|Newton|Joule|Watt|Kelvin|Force is measured in newtons. Joule measures energy or work, watt measures power and kelvin measures thermodynamic temperature.
13|When a constant force acts along a displacement how is work calculated?|Force multiplied by displacement|Force divided by displacement|Mass divided by current|Temperature multiplied by charge|For force parallel to motion, work is W = Fd. The displacement must be in the force direction; the result is expressed in joules.
14|What is the SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature?|Kelvin|Celsius|Joule|Watt|Kelvin is the SI base unit. Degrees Celsius are commonly used operationally, but are not the SI base unit.
15|Heat is best described as what?|Energy transferred because of a temperature difference|An intrinsic mass of electrons|The number of protons|Electrical resistance alone|Heat is energy in transit from higher to lower temperature. Temperature describes thermal state, while heat describes a transfer process.
17|Which subatomic particle carries positive charge?|Proton|Neutron|Electron|Photon|A proton has positive elementary charge. A neutron is electrically neutral, an electron is negative and an X-ray photon carries no electric charge.
17|What determines the atomic number of an element?|Number of protons|Number of neutrons alone|Number of electron shells|Total number of nucleons|Atomic number Z is the proton count and identifies the element. Protons plus neutrons determine mass number A.
18|What is the maximum occupancy of the first electron shell?|2 electrons|8 electrons|18 electrons|32 electrons|The first shell holds up to two electrons. Higher shells have different capacities; shell occupancy should not be confused with atomic number.
19|How is mass number defined?|Protons plus neutrons|Protons minus electrons|Neutrons minus protons|Electrons plus photons|The mass number counts nucleons: A = Z + N. Electrons contribute negligible mass relative to nucleons and are not included in mass number.
19|Which electrons primarily govern chemical bonding?|Valence electrons|Only nuclear neutrons|Only inner protons|X-ray photons|Valence electrons occupy the outermost shell and govern bonding and chemical behavior. Nuclear composition instead defines the element and its isotopes.
22|Isotopes of the same element differ in what?|Number of neutrons|Number of protons|Sign of proton charge|Identity of the element|Isotopes have the same proton number but different neutron counts, giving different mass numbers. A different proton count would define a different element.
23|What is ionization?|Gain or loss of electrons producing net charge|An electron moving up a level while remaining bound only|A change in room temperature only|A neutron becoming a detector pixel|Ionization creates an ion by electron loss or gain. Excitation raises a bound electron to a higher energy state without necessarily removing it.
24|What distinguishes excitation from ionization?|The electron remains bound to the atom|A proton always leaves the nucleus|All electrons are permanently removed|Atomic number always changes|Excitation places a bound electron at a higher energy level. Ionization involves electron removal or gain and a change in net charge.
25|What is the SI unit of electric current?|Ampere|Volt|Farad|Ohm|Electric current is charge flow per unit time, measured in amperes. One ampere equals one coulomb per second.
26|Potential difference is measured in which unit?|Volt|Ampere|Gray|Becquerel|Voltage represents electrical energy per unit charge and is measured in volts. Current uses amperes and radiation quantities use different units.
27|What is the SI unit of capacitance?|Farad|Ohm|Watt|Tesla|Capacitance expresses charge stored per unit potential difference and is measured in farads. Resistance is measured in ohms.
28|Electromagnetic induction depends on which condition?|Changing magnetic flux|Constant zero magnetic flux only|Absence of all conductors|Static temperature alone|A changing magnetic flux through a circuit induces electromotive force. Faster flux change or more coil turns increases induced voltage under Faraday’s law.
29|What is the SI unit of resistance?|Ohm|Volt|Ampere|Coulomb|Resistance describes opposition to current flow and is measured in ohms. Voltage, current and charge have distinct SI units.
30|A 12 V source is connected across 4 ohms. Calculate current.|3 A|48 A|0.33 A|8 A|Ohm’s law gives I = V/R. Substituting 12/4 gives 3 A; multiplying the values would give the wrong physical quantity.
30|Which equation states Ohm’s law?|V = IR|V = I/R|R = VI|I = VR|Voltage equals current multiplied by resistance for an ohmic conductor under the relevant physical conditions. Rearrangements are I = V/R and R = V/I.
31|Three resistors of 100 20 and 30 ohms are in series. What is total resistance?|150 ohms|12.5 ohms|50 ohms|60000 ohms|Series resistances add directly: 100 + 20 + 30 = 150 ohms. The same current flows through each resistor.
32|Two 50-ohm resistors are in parallel. What is their equivalent resistance?|25 ohms|100 ohms|50 ohms|2500 ohms|For two parallel resistors, R = R1R2/(R1+R2). Thus 2500/100 = 25 ohms, less than either branch resistance.
34|A 100-ohm resistor is in series with two parallel 50-ohm resistors. What is the total?|125 ohms|200 ohms|25 ohms|75 ohms|The parallel pair is 25 ohms. Adding the 100-ohm series resistor gives a total of 125 ohms.
35|What is the SI unit of energy?|Joule|Watt|Ampere|Volt|Energy is measured in joules. A watt is a joule per second and describes power rather than total energy.
36|A lamp uses 60 joules every second. What is its power?|60 W|1 W|3600 W|0.017 W|Power is energy transferred per unit time: P = E/t. Sixty joules per second is sixty watts.
38|According to Joule’s heating law doubling current changes heat by what factor if resistance and time are constant?|Four times|Twice|Half|Unchanged|H = I²Rt, so doubling current multiplies I² by four. Resistance and duration must be held constant for this comparison.
40|How does a fuse interrupt an excessive current?|Its element heats and melts|It stores all current as a magnetic image|It chemically fixes the circuit|It increases circuit voltage indefinitely|Excess current produces Joule heating that melts the fuse element and opens the circuit. This prevents continued current flow through the protected circuit.
42|Which energy source uses photovoltaic cells?|Solar energy|Nuclear fission|Wind alone|Coal combustion|Photovoltaic cells convert light into electrical energy. Wind and hydropower instead drive turbines, while nuclear and fossil systems typically use thermal generation.
44|What does electromotive force describe?|Energy supplied per unit charge by a source|Energy absorbed per kilogram of tissue|Nuclear decays per second|Resistance per unit length only|EMF describes energy a source supplies to each unit of charge and is measured in volts. Absorbed dose and activity are radiation quantities, not electrical EMF.
48|How do X-ray photons behave in an electric field?|They are not deflected by charge forces|They bend toward the positive plate as electrons do|They acquire proton mass|They always stop immediately|X-ray photons have no electric charge, so electric fields do not deflect them like charged particles. Their propagation and interactions differ from electron motion.
48|What is the approximate speed of X-rays in vacuum?|3 × 10⁸ m/s|3 × 10³ m/s|300 m/s|3 m/s|X-rays are electromagnetic radiation and travel at the speed of light in vacuum. Their short wavelength does not make them travel faster than visible light in vacuum.
50|Thermionic emission occurs at which component?|Heated cathode filament|Cold collimator mirror|Film base|PACS workstation|Heating the cathode filament releases electrons that form a space-charge cloud. High voltage then accelerates these electrons toward the anode.
51|Approximately what fraction of electron energy becomes heat at a diagnostic X-ray target?|About 99 percent|About 1 percent|Exactly zero|About 10 percent|The lecture describes roughly 99% heat and about 1% X-ray production under typical diagnostic conditions. This low efficiency makes target heat management essential.
52|What type of spectrum is produced by bremsstrahlung?|Continuous energy spectrum|A single fixed energy only|Only visible red light|No photon emission|Different electron decelerations release different photon energies, creating a continuous spectrum. Characteristic radiation instead has discrete energies tied to atomic shell differences.
53|Characteristic X-ray energy is determined by what?|Difference between atomic shell binding energies|Patient identification number|Film hanger length|Room humidity alone|An electron transition into an inner-shell vacancy releases the difference in binding energies as a photon. This yields energies characteristic of the target element.''')
add(8,'''8|How many carpal bones are present in one hand?|8|5|7|14|The wrist has eight carpal bones in two rows. One hand also has five metacarpals and fourteen phalanges, which are separate bone groups.
8|Which sequence describes the proximal carpal row from lateral to medial?|Scaphoid lunate triquetrum pisiform|Trapezium trapezoid capitate hamate|Pisiform hamate capitate scaphoid|Lunate trapezium hamate trapezoid|The proximal row is scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum and pisiform. Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate and hamate form the distal row.
8|Which carpal belongs to the distal row?|Capitate|Scaphoid|Lunate|Pisiform|Capitate is in the distal row with trapezium, trapezoid and hamate. The other options belong to the proximal row.
9|Which muscle is an intrinsic shoulder muscle?|Deltoid|Trapezius|Latissimus dorsi|Rhomboid major|Deltoid is part of the intrinsic shoulder group together with teres major and rotator cuff muscles. The other listed muscles are extrinsic shoulder muscles.
11|Which bones articulate at the glenohumeral joint?|Humerus and scapula|Radius and ulna|Clavicle and sternum|Humerus and radius only|The humeral head articulates with the glenoid cavity of the scapula. This is the ball-and-socket shoulder joint.
11|Which structure deepens the glenoid cavity?|Glenoid labrum|Patellar ligament|Tibial meniscus|Annular ligament of the radius|The fibrocartilaginous glenoid labrum increases the depth of the shallow glenoid socket. This supports stability while allowing substantial shoulder movement.
12|What is a major stabilizing muscle group at the shoulder?|Rotator cuff|Quadriceps|Hamstrings|Triceps surae|The rotator cuff provides dynamic support to the shoulder joint. Quadriceps, hamstrings and triceps surae are lower-limb groups.
13|Which muscle initiates shoulder abduction?|Supraspinatus|Latissimus dorsi|Pectoralis major|Subscapularis|Supraspinatus initiates abduction, after which deltoid is the principal contributor. Latissimus dorsi and pectoralis major chiefly adduct, while subscapularis medially rotates.
13|Which muscle is a principal shoulder extensor?|Latissimus dorsi|Pectoralis minor|Serratus anterior|Subclavius|Latissimus dorsi is a chief extensor and is assisted by posterior deltoid fibres. The other muscles primarily position or stabilize the pectoral girdle.
14|Which nerve supplies deltoid and is relevant in shoulder injury?|Axillary nerve|Femoral nerve|Obturator nerve|Tibial nerve|The axillary nerve is among the nerves related to the shoulder and supplies deltoid. Its anatomical relationship makes it important when assessing shoulder trauma.
17|Where does pectoralis minor insert?|Coracoid process|Olecranon|Radial styloid|Tibial tuberosity|Pectoralis minor arises from upper ribs and inserts on the coracoid process of the scapula. The olecranon and radial styloid are distal upper-limb landmarks.
17|What is a principal action of pectoralis major?|Adduction and medial rotation of the arm|Dorsiflexion of the ankle|Extension of the toes|Pronation of the foot|Pectoralis major draws the arm toward the body and rotates it medially. Its action is at the shoulder, not the lower limb.
18|Serratus anterior inserts mainly on which scapular surface?|Costal surface of the medial border|Lateral clavicular end|Humeral capitulum|Olecranon fossa|The muscle attaches to the costal aspect of the medial scapular border and helps position the scapula against the thoracic wall.
21|Which nerve supplies the anterior compartment of the arm?|Musculocutaneous nerve|Radial nerve|Tibial nerve|Common fibular nerve|The anterior arm contains biceps brachii, brachialis and coracobrachialis and is supplied chiefly by the musculocutaneous nerve. The posterior compartment is supplied by the radial nerve.
21|Which muscle is in the posterior arm compartment?|Triceps brachii|Biceps brachii|Coracobrachialis|Brachialis|Triceps brachii is an elbow extensor in the posterior compartment, supplied by the radial nerve. The other options are anterior arm muscles.
23|Which structure connects the shafts of radius and ulna?|Interosseous membrane|Glenoid labrum|Patellar tendon|Spring ligament|The interosseous membrane joins the two forearm bones and helps transfer forces between them. The glenoid labrum belongs to the shoulder.
24|The common flexor origin is at which humeral landmark?|Medial epicondyle|Lateral epicondyle|Greater tubercle|Anatomical neck|The anterior forearm flexor-pronator group arises in part from the medial epicondyle. The common extensor origin is on the lateral epicondyle.
25|Which muscle flexes the elbow despite lying in the forearm’s posterior compartment and receiving radial nerve supply?|Brachioradialis|Flexor digitorum superficialis|Flexor carpi radialis|Palmaris longus|Brachioradialis flexes the elbow but lies in the posterior compartment and has radial nerve supply. This is an exception to a simplistic flexor-versus-extensor innervation rule.
26|Which nerve supplies the posterior forearm extensor group?|Radial nerve|Obturator nerve|Femoral nerve|Musculocutaneous nerve only|The posterior extensor-supinator group is supplied by the radial nerve and its branches. These muscles commonly arise from the lateral epicondyle.
27|Compression of which nerve causes carpal tunnel syndrome?|Median nerve|Axillary nerve|Femoral nerve|Tibial nerve|The median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel beneath the flexor retinaculum. Compression can impair its sensory and motor functions; the other nerves do not traverse this tunnel.
28|Which artery crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox?|Radial artery|Femoral artery|Popliteal artery|Posterior tibial artery|The radial artery winds around the lateral wrist and crosses the snuffbox floor to reach the hand. This is an important surface-anatomy relationship.
31|The thenar compartment is located at the base of which digit?|Thumb|Little finger|Middle finger|Ring finger|Thenar muscles form the thumb base. The hypothenar compartment is at the base of the little finger.
31|The hypothenar compartment is associated with which digit?|Little finger|Thumb|Index finger|Middle finger|Hypothenar muscles act on the little finger. The thumb has the distinct thenar muscle group.
33|Why are hand fascial spaces clinically important?|They help localize infection and pus collections|They produce X-rays|They replace all tendons|They store silver halide|Fascial septa define potential spaces that influence the spread and localization of infection. Knowledge of these spaces helps explain the pattern of hand infections.''')
add(9,'''7|Teaching and specialist hospitals represent which level of health care?|Tertiary|Primary|Community self-care only|Informal care|The lecture groups clinics and health centres as primary, general hospitals as secondary and teaching or specialist hospitals as tertiary care.
10|Which quality best supports good radiographic patient care?|Empathy and attention to detail|Ignoring patient questions|Rushing every transfer|Working outside scope|Empathy supports the patient’s emotional needs, while accuracy and attention to detail protect technical quality and safety. Ignoring concerns undermines both care and communication.
12|Which ethical principle respects a patient’s choices?|Autonomy|Non-maleficence only|Equipment efficiency|Stock rotation|Autonomy recognizes the patient’s right to make informed choices. Beneficence concerns acting for good and non-maleficence concerns avoiding harm.
12|Which principle means acting for the patient’s good?|Beneficence|Autonomy only|Archiving|Image cropping|Beneficence directs care toward the patient’s welfare. It complements autonomy, non-maleficence and justice rather than replacing them.
12|Which ethical principle means do no harm?|Non-maleficence|Justice only|Confidentiality alone|Replenishment|Non-maleficence requires avoiding preventable harm, including unnecessary radiation exposure. Justice addresses fairness and confidentiality protects private information.
12|Treating every patient fairly expresses which principle?|Justice|Thermionic emission|Image enhancement|Stock control|Justice concerns fairness in care and allocation of services. It is an ethical principle, not a technical image-production process.
14|What should be confirmed before any patient exposure?|Patient identity|Only the room number|Only the cassette brand|Only the time on the wall clock|Confirming identity helps prevent wrong-patient and wrong-examination errors. The request and intended examination must be matched to the patient before exposure.
17|What is an appropriate approach to an anxious patient?|Explain each step and allow questions|Make promises that discomfort is impossible|Ignore visible fear|Use unexplained technical jargon|Clear honest explanation and opportunities for questions reduce uncertainty. False reassurance and unexplained jargon can increase anxiety and undermine trust.
18|Which is a patient right?|Refusal of an examination|Automatic public release of records|Compulsory agreement without information|Loss of privacy during every procedure|Patients have rights to information, dignity, privacy and refusal. Respecting refusal requires appropriate communication and documentation within the clinical context.
19|What is essential for informed consent?|Understanding the purpose benefits and risks|A signature without explanation|Only a printed appointment time|A technician deciding without discussion|Consent depends on meaningful understanding and a voluntary choice. A signature alone does not demonstrate that the patient understood the procedure.
21|Which behavior best preserves confidentiality?|Protect records and avoid public case discussions|Discuss identifiable cases in public corridors|Share passwords with visitors|Publish identifiable images without authority|Confidentiality requires secure handling and appropriate information sharing. Public discussion or unauthorized disclosure can damage trust and expose private patient information.
23|Which history item is particularly relevant before contrast administration?|Allergy history|Favorite food alone|Shoe size alone|Preferred phone brand|The lecture emphasizes allergies as part of patient history and checking relevant risks before contrast. History should be assessed with the responsible clinical team and protocol.
24|What should be done with abnormal vital signs?|Record and report them promptly|Ignore them if the image is needed|Delete them from the record|Assume they are always equipment errors|Vital signs reflect basic physiological function. Abnormal readings need timely reporting and appropriate clinical assessment rather than being ignored or removed.
25|Which resting adult pulse range is given in the lecture?|60–100 beats per minute|10–20 beats per minute|150–200 beats per minute|220–280 beats per minute|The lecture gives 60–100 beats per minute as a normal adult reference range. Clinical interpretation still depends on the patient and circumstances.
25|Which adult respiratory rate range is given in the lecture?|12–20 breaths per minute|2–4 breaths per minute|40–60 breaths per minute|80–100 breaths per minute|The reference adult respiratory range is 12–20 breaths per minute. Rate must be interpreted alongside clinical signs and the patient’s baseline.
26|When hands are visibly soiled which hygiene method is appropriate?|Soap and water|Alcohol rub as the only method regardless of soiling|Wiping on a uniform|Putting gloves over dirt only|The lecture recommends washing with soap and water and allows alcohol rub when hands are not visibly soiled. Gloves do not replace hand hygiene.
27|What is the basis of standard precautions?|Treat every patient as potentially infectious|Use precautions only for known infections|Reuse contaminated sharps|Recap every used needle|Standard precautions apply to all patients because infection status may be unknown. They include appropriate PPE, sharps safety and waste management.
27|What should follow a needle-stick injury?|Immediate reporting and local exposure protocol|Concealment until the next month|Recapping the needle and continuing silently|Discarding the incident record|Prompt reporting allows assessment and timely post-exposure management according to local procedure. Concealment delays care and prevents proper incident review.
31|Which measure is particularly appropriate for an elderly patient?|Allow time and prevent falls|Force rapid movement|Ignore hearing difficulty|Remove all positioning support|Older patients may have fragile skin, limited mobility or sensory impairment. Allowing extra time and preventing falls supports safe positioning and transfer.
33|When transfer of a critically ill patient is unsafe what imaging approach may be appropriate?|Mobile X-ray with coordinated monitoring|Disconnect all monitors and transfer alone|Ignore airway status|Remove essential lines|The lecture supports mobile imaging when transfer is unsafe and coordination with nursing staff while maintaining airway, lines and monitoring.
35|What does ALARA stand for?|As Low As Reasonably Achievable|As Large As Radiographs Allow|All Light Automatically Reduced Always|Anatomical Level And Radiation Angle|ALARA is an optimisation principle: keep exposures as low as reasonably achievable while meeting the clinical imaging objective. It does not mean sacrificing necessary diagnostic information.
35|Which three tools are emphasized for radiation protection?|Time distance and shielding|Humidity brightness and font size|Fixing washing and printing|Temperature rotation and storage|Reducing exposure time, increasing distance and using appropriate shielding are core protective tools. Their use must fit the specific examination and working conditions.
36|Which action helps avoid unnecessary repeat exposure?|Correct positioning and appropriate exposure selection|Skipping patient identification|Increasing every exposure without review|Ignoring image quality|Accurate positioning and suitable technical factors improve the chance of a diagnostic first image. Avoidable repeats increase dose without adding intended benefit.
37|What device monitors occupational radiation exposure?|Personal dosimeter|Densitometer|Film hopper|Clinical thermometer|A personal dosimeter tracks occupational exposure. A densitometer measures film optical density and a thermometer measures temperature.
40|A patient collapses with airway swelling during contrast injection. What is the immediate response described?|Stop injection and call for emergency help|Continue injection to finish the dose|Leave the patient alone|Wait until the next appointment|Collapse and airway swelling indicate a severe reaction requiring urgent assistance. Stop the injection, activate emergency response and follow the local emergency protocol and professional scope.
42|What is health management in a radiology service?|Planning organization and coordination for safe efficient care|Only buying the most expensive machine|Only storing unlabelled films|Avoiding all team communication|Management coordinates resources, people and workflow to support safe and efficient service. It includes more than equipment purchasing.
45|Which activity is part of radiographic quality assurance?|Image reject analysis|Ignoring all repeated films|Removing maintenance logs|Using unchecked equipment indefinitely|Reject analysis identifies patterns of image failure and opportunities to reduce repeats. QA also includes equipment tests, patient feedback and audit.
46|What supports continuing professional development?|Training updates and reflection on practice|Avoiding all refresher teaching|Relying only on outdated notes|Working beyond competence|Ongoing education and reflection help maintain competence as equipment, protocols and professional expectations change. Development is part of safe continuing practice.''')
add(0,'''8|Which is the main artery supplying the lower limb?|Femoral artery|External carotid artery|Brachial artery|Radial artery|The femoral artery is the major arterial supply of the lower limb. It continues from the external iliac artery beneath the inguinal ligament. The source slide says “external carotid”; this is a source typographical error. The supplied slide wording remains unchanged.
11|Which muscles are important abductors of the hip?|Gluteus medius and minimus|Adductor longus and brevis|Biceps brachii and brachialis|Gastrocnemius and soleus|Gluteus medius and minimus abduct the thigh and help stabilize the pelvis. The adductor group draws the thigh toward the midline; the other pairs act in different regions.
16|Femoral neck fractures are clinically associated with which concern?|Avascular necrosis of the femoral head|Carpal tunnel compression|Rotator cuff tearing|Static film artefact|The lecture identifies avascular necrosis as a key concern in femoral neck injury because blood supply to the head can be compromised. The other conditions relate to different anatomy or film processing.
21|A Pott-type ankle fracture-dislocation may follow which mechanism described in the lecture?|Forced eversion|Isolated wrist extension|Shoulder abduction|Finger opposition|Forced eversion can stress the medial ligament and injure malleolar structures. The lecture describes the resulting ankle fracture-dislocation pattern; the other movements do not act at the ankle.
25|Which leg compartment primarily performs dorsiflexion?|Anterior compartment|Superficial posterior compartment|Lateral compartment only|Gluteal compartment|The anterior compartment contains dorsiflexors and toe extensors such as tibialis anterior. The posterior group chiefly plantarflexes, while lateral fibular muscles chiefly evert.
25|Which pair primarily plantarflexes the foot?|Gastrocnemius and soleus|Tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus|Biceps brachii and brachialis|Gluteus medius and minimus|Gastrocnemius and soleus are posterior leg muscles that plantarflex the ankle. Tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus belong to the dorsiflexor group.
25|Which muscles are the principal lateral-compartment evertors?|Fibularis longus and brevis|Tibialis anterior and posterior|Semitendinosus and semimembranosus|Rectus femoris and sartorius|Fibularis longus and brevis occupy the lateral leg compartment and evert the foot. The hamstrings and anterior thigh muscles are not lateral leg evertors.
26|Which muscle forms the superolateral boundary of the popliteal fossa?|Biceps femoris|Adductor longus|Rectus femoris|Tibialis anterior|Biceps femoris bounds the fossa superolaterally. Semimembranosus and semitendinosus form the superomedial boundary, and gastrocnemius contributes the inferior boundaries.
28|How many phalanges are present in a normal foot?|14|5|8|7|The forefoot has five metatarsals and fourteen phalanges. These should not be confused with the seven tarsal bones in the hindfoot and midfoot.
30|Which bones form the lateral longitudinal arch?|Calcaneus cuboid and fourth and fifth metatarsals|Talus navicular and first metatarsal only|Scaphoid lunate and capitate|Femur tibia and patella|The lateral longitudinal arch includes the calcaneus, cuboid and lateral two metatarsals. The medial arch instead includes talus, navicular and the first three metatarsals.''')
add(1,'''17|Compared with direct-exposure film why can a screen-film system use less X-ray exposure?|Screens convert X-rays to light efficiently|Screens remove all radiation before imaging|Screen-film needs no latent image|The base becomes opaque before exposure|Intensifying screens convert absorbed X-ray energy into light that exposes compatible film. This amplification can reduce the X-ray exposure required compared with direct-exposure film.
18|Which film receives a larger proportion of exposure directly from X-rays?|Direct-exposure film|Screen film alone|Duplicating film only|Slide reversal film only|Direct-exposure film relies more directly on X-ray interactions. Screen film is designed mainly for the light emitted by intensifying screens.
20|Which dental film is positioned inside the mouth?|Intraoral film|Extraoral film|CT hard-copy sheet|Mass chest roll film|Intraoral film is small and placed within a light-tight packet inside the mouth. Extraoral film is positioned outside, commonly in a cassette.
22|Which listed metric film size is used for a large standard radiographic sheet?|35 × 43 cm|3 × 4 cm|2 × 2 cm|1 × 1 cm|The lecture lists 35 × 43 cm among common radiographic film sizes. The much smaller options are not that large standard sheet size.
29|Bending film during handling is associated with which artefact?|Crescent or crimp marks|Perfectly uniform sharpening|A DICOM header error|Grid cutoff alone|Mechanical flexing or kinking can produce black or white crescent marks. Careful edge handling and avoiding bending reduce these pressure-related artefacts.
31|Which roll-film size is listed for mass chest screening?|100 mm|1 mm|250 mm|500 mm|The lecture associates 100 mm roll film with mass chest screening and 105 mm with mass miniature radiography. These are historical film-based recording applications.
32|Which photographic material is used to copy an existing radiograph?|Duplicating film|Unexposed lead foil|Developer restrainer|Dry polyester base alone|Duplicating film reproduces existing radiographs for referral, teaching or records. It does not require the patient to undergo a new exposure merely to make that copy.
34|Which screen material is associated with blue-sensitive film in the lecture?|Calcium tungstate|Amorphous selenium|Sodium thiosulfate|Acetic acid|Calcium tungstate emits light compatible with blue-sensitive film. The screen emission must match the film response for efficient imaging.
36|Which film category is sensitive across much of the visible spectrum including red?|Panchromatic film|Blue-sensitive film only|Orthochromatic green-sensitive film only|Non-photosensitive polyester base|Panchromatic film responds across a broad visible spectrum. Because red light may also expose it, ordinary radiographic safelight assumptions cannot simply be applied.
42|What is a limitation of instant photographic film described in the lecture?|Poor long-term stability|Unlimited archival permanence|No cost per print|Ability to replace all digital storage|Instant prints offer rapid output but may have limited long-term stability and higher cost per print. Their convenience should be distinguished from archival durability.''')
add(2,'''15|Which software category includes Windows and Linux?|Operating systems|Contrast media|Photostimulable phosphors|Developer agents|Windows and Linux are operating systems that manage hardware and support applications. They are distinct from clinical acquisition or image-storage applications.
14|Which is an example of DICOM viewing software named in the lecture?|Weasis|Microsoft Word|Microsoft Excel|LibreOffice Writer|Weasis is named as a DICOM viewer for medical images. Word and Writer are document tools, while Excel is a spreadsheet.
21|Source-to-image distance belongs to which data category?|Technical data|Patient surname|Diagnostic conclusion only|Administrative leave record|SID is an acquisition parameter, grouped with kVp, mA, mAs and focal-spot size as technical data. It differs from demographic or interpreted diagnostic data.
24|Which quality means information is protected against unauthorized access?|Confidentiality|Magnification|Pixel pitch|Film speed|Confidentiality protects patient information from inappropriate access or disclosure. It applies to images, reports and associated records.
34|Why should files be organized with patient identifiers examination dates and types?|To support accurate retrieval and comparison|To remove the need for backups|To guarantee no storage failure|To make every image anonymous automatically|Consistent file organization helps staff locate the correct examination and compare prior studies. It does not itself create backups or anonymize data.
42|Can digital communication occur without the public internet?|Yes through a private hospital network|No every transfer must be public|Only when data are printed|Only if patient identifiers are removed from all records|Digital communication is electronic information exchange and can occur on a private local network. The internet is one means of connectivity, not a requirement for every digital transfer.
44|What is the purpose of reference-management software?|Organize sources and format citations|Generate X-ray tube current|Chemically develop film|Measure absorbed radiation dose|Reference managers store bibliographic details and help format citations and reference lists. They support research writing rather than image acquisition.
45|Which resource primarily searches biomedical literature?|PubMed|A film hopper|A densitometer|A collimator|PubMed is listed for searching biomedical and health research. The other options are physical radiography or processing tools.
45|Which application is specifically listed for organizing research references?|Zotero|A TFT array|A film processor|A high-tension transformer|Zotero manages references and supports citation workflows. It does not operate detector readout or X-ray generation.
45|Which organization is listed as a radiation-protection and medical-imaging information resource?|IAEA|A generic social-media feed|An unverified file-sharing account|A film-manufacturer logo alone|The International Atomic Energy Agency is listed as a resource for radiation protection and medical imaging. Source authority and relevance should be assessed when conducting online research.
47|Why should students critically assess online research?|Some information is false outdated or methodologically weak|Every online source is automatically peer reviewed|Search rank guarantees scientific accuracy|All websites follow the same clinical standard|The lecture highlights misinformation, outdated studies and weak methods as research challenges. Students should assess evidence quality rather than assume search visibility proves reliability.
47|Copying research text without proper acknowledgment risks what?|Plagiarism|Thermionic emission|Grid cutoff|Film reticulation|Using another author’s work without appropriate acknowledgment is plagiarism. Correct citation and responsible paraphrasing are essential research practices.''')
add(3,'''9|Which is a benefit of daylight film systems?|Normal-light handling within a protected enclosure|Unlimited unprotected white-light exposure|Elimination of every chemical process|Direct conversion of all film into PACS data|The light-tight loading system allows routine handling in a normally illuminated room. It still protects sensitive film and still uses conventional chemical processing.
20|During CR readout what does a light detector convert emitted light into?|An electrical signal|An acid fixer bath|A lead grid|A patient appointment|Light from the stimulated phosphor is collected and detected, producing an electrical signal for subsequent digitization. This is separate from plate erasure.
24|Which is an advantage of CR in an existing radiography department?|Compatibility with many existing X-ray units|No need for any plate maintenance|Guaranteed zero patient dose|No possibility of artefacts|CR can often be introduced using existing X-ray equipment because the cassette-based phosphor plate replaces film. Plates still need care, cleaning and erasure.
25|Which CR plate problem can produce image artefacts?|Scratches dust or contamination|Correct routine erasure|Clean intact plate surfaces|Proper handling|Physical damage and contamination interfere with stored or detected image signals. Regular maintenance and careful handling reduce avoidable artefacts.
28|Which DR conversion sequence uses a scintillator?|X-rays to light to electrical charge|X-rays directly to printed report text|Visible light directly to fixer|Electrical charge to silver-halide crystals|Indirect DR first converts X-rays into light in a scintillator and then converts the light to charge in photodiodes. Direct conversion produces charge without that light stage.
32|Which conversion material avoids the intermediate light stage in DR?|Amorphous selenium|Cesium iodide scintillator|Gadolinium oxysulfide scintillator|Calcium tungstate screen|Amorphous selenium is a direct-conversion photoconductor. The other listed materials are associated with converting X-ray energy into light.
38|After detector signals are read what forms the displayed digital radiograph?|Computer processing of numerical image data|Chemical fixing of a CR cassette|A film hanger alone|Room-light exposure of a detector|The detector signals are digitized and processed by a computer into a displayable image. Digital image formation does not require routine wet film processing.
42|What does edge enhancement primarily emphasize?|Boundaries between structures|Patient identification accuracy|Fixer strength|Room shielding thickness|Edge enhancement increases the visibility of changes between adjacent image regions. Excessive processing can mislead, so presentation should remain appropriate to the diagnostic task.
44|What is dose creep in digital imaging?|Gradual unnecessary increase in exposure masked by acceptable image appearance|A fixed mandatory reduction to zero dose|Radioactive contamination from PACS|Physical motion of a lead apron|Wide exposure latitude and display normalization may make overexposure less obvious than on film. Monitoring exposure indicators and appropriate technique helps control gradual increases.
45|Why do digital imaging systems need reliable backups?|Image data can be lost through failures or cyberattacks|Digital storage can never fail|Backups increase patient dose|Backup files replace all confidentiality controls|Hardware, software and security failures can compromise stored images. Secure recoverable backups support continuity but must still protect confidentiality.''')
add(4,'''5|Why is anode rotation useful in a rotating-anode tube?|Distribute target heat over a larger track|Stop all electron production|Replace filtration|Remove all need for tube cooling|Rotation spreads electron bombardment across a focal track rather than concentrating heat at one stationary spot. Heat management remains essential because most electron energy becomes heat.
15|What is an advantage of a high-frequency generator described in the lecture?|Nearly constant tube voltage|Chemical film fixation|Automatic patient consent|No need for electrical power|Electronic switching produces a nearly constant tube voltage and efficient consistent operation. The generator still requires power and appropriate exposure control.
23|Which technique can reduce scatter reaching the image receptor?|Air-gap technique|Increasing field size without limit|Removing all collimation|Ignoring grid alignment|An air gap allows more divergent scattered photons to miss the receptor. Collimation and appropriate grid use are other scatter-control approaches.
24|What material forms the absorbing strips of a conventional anti-scatter grid?|Lead|Gelatin|Polyester alone|Air only|Lead strips absorb off-axis radiation, while radiolucent interspaces permit aligned primary radiation to pass. Grid alignment and appropriate exposure technique remain important.
25|What is a crossed grid?|Two linear grids superimposed|A single film hanger|A rotating anode disk|A split developer tank|A crossed grid combines two linear grids to reject scatter in two directions. Its geometry differs from a single linear grid.
28|Mammography equipment is specialized for imaging which anatomy?|Breast|Only the ankle|Only the wrist|Only the femoral shaft|Mammography is a dedicated breast X-ray imaging system used for detection and evaluation of breast disease. It is not a general-purpose extremity-only system.''')
add(5,'''19|Before processing patient films after startup what condition should be reached?|Stable operating temperature|An empty wash-water tank|Unlabelled replenisher containers|A deliberately open drain valve|The processor needs time to reach a stable operating condition before patient films are introduced. Tank levels, water supply and drain status also require checks.
21|Damp film at processor exit should prompt which check?|Dryer performance and fixer condition|Only the report font|Only the PACS password|Only the room clock|Inadequate drying heat, exhausted fixer or hardener issues can contribute to wet film. Troubleshooting should check the relevant processor systems rather than unrelated workstation settings.
22|Why should actual developer temperature be measured independently?|To verify the displayed reading|To replace sensitometry permanently|To calculate patient age|To remove all replenishment|An independent check can reveal an inaccurate temperature display or control problem. Temperature verification complements control-film measurements.
23|How can replenishment delivery be checked?|Measure the volume pumped for a known film workload|Count the letters in a report|Measure the patient wrist circumference|Inspect only the monitor brightness|Comparing actual delivered volume with the specified replenishment setting checks pump performance. The lecture includes this as a weekly QA activity.''')
add(8,'''4|The brachial plexus commonly arises from which spinal nerve roots?|C5–T1|L1–L5|S2–S4|T8–T12|The lecture identifies ventral rami C5 through T1 as the roots of the brachial plexus. This plexus supplies the upper limb.
6|Which bone is described as the first to begin ossification?|Clavicle|Patella|Scaphoid|Calcaneus|The lecture notes that clavicular ossification begins early in intrauterine life. This feature distinguishes it from the other bones listed.
7|The humeral capitulum articulates with which structure?|Head of the radius|Head of the femur|Talus|Scaphoid|The distal humeral capitulum lies laterally and articulates with the radial head. The trochlea lies medially and articulates with the ulna.
7|The humeral trochlea articulates with which forearm bone?|Ulna|Radius only|Clavicle|Scapula|The medial pulley-shaped trochlea forms the humeroulnar articulation. The lateral capitulum receives the radial head.''')
add(9,'''6|Which needs are included in holistic patient care in the lecture?|Physical emotional and social needs|Only image brightness|Only equipment maintenance|Only appointment billing|Patient care addresses the person’s physical, emotional and social needs. Technical image quality is essential but is only one part of the overall care experience.
13|What is an essential professional boundary for a technician?|Work within the authorized scope of practice|Perform any intervention without training|Ignore departmental procedures|Replace all specialist decisions independently|Professional practice requires understanding duties, competence and authorized scope. Team members should seek appropriate assistance for tasks outside that scope.
28|Which is an appropriate equipment-cleaning approach?|Use approved disinfectant on relevant surfaces|Ignore contaminated contact surfaces|Use any unlabelled chemical|Clean only when an infection is confirmed|Tables, cassettes and equipment contact surfaces require appropriate cleaning with approved agents. Standard precautions do not depend on knowing a patient’s infection status.''')
add(0,'''9|Which landmark separates the two buttocks?|Intergluteal cleft|Inguinal canal|Popliteal crease|Tibial crest|The intergluteal cleft separates the buttocks. The gluteal fold instead marks the inferior boundary of the gluteal region.
9|Which fold marks the superior boundary of the thigh posteriorly?|Gluteal fold|Axillary fold|Cubital crease|Plantar crease|The gluteal fold separates the gluteal region from the posterior thigh. The other folds belong to different anatomical regions.
12|Relative to piriformis, where does the superior gluteal artery leave the pelvis?|Superior to the muscle|Inferior to the muscle|Through the obturator canal|Through the femoral canal|The superior gluteal artery passes above piriformis through the greater sciatic foramen. The inferior gluteal artery passes below it.
12|Which opening transmits the internal pudendal artery into the perineum?|Lesser sciatic foramen|Obturator canal|Femoral ring|Inguinal canal|After entering the gluteal region through the greater sciatic foramen, the internal pudendal artery enters the perineum through the lesser sciatic foramen.
14|Which group consists entirely of hip capsular ligaments?|Iliofemoral pubofemoral and ischiofemoral|Patellar annular and cruciate|Coracoacromial coracohumeral and annular|Deltoid plantar and collateral|The lecture identifies these three thickenings of the hip joint capsule. The distractor groups contain ligaments of other joints.
14|What vascular structure is carried by the ligament of the head of the femur?|A small artery to the femoral head|The femoral vein|The popliteal artery|The great saphenous vein|The ligament contains a small artery supplying the femoral head. It does not carry the major vessels listed in the other options.
17|How does the patella assist the quadriceps mechanism?|Increases quadriceps leverage|Prevents all knee movement|Forms the ankle mortise|Replaces the tibial plateau|The patella slides over the anterior distal femur and increases quadriceps leverage. It assists the knee extensor mechanism rather than immobilizing the joint.
24|Which structure forms the superior boundary of the femoral triangle?|Inguinal ligament|Patellar ligament|Sacrotuberous ligament|Achilles tendon|The inguinal ligament forms the superior boundary. Sartorius and adductor longus form its lateral and medial boundaries respectively.
26|Which muscles form the superomedial boundary of the popliteal fossa?|Semimembranosus and semitendinosus|Sartorius and gracilis|Rectus femoris and vastus lateralis|Tibialis anterior and fibularis longus|The two medial hamstrings form the superomedial boundary. Biceps femoris forms the superolateral boundary.
29|Which tendon supports the transverse arch of the foot in the lecture?|Fibularis longus tendon|Biceps brachii tendon|Quadriceps tendon|Triceps brachii tendon|The transverse arch is supported by the fibularis longus tendon and interosseous and plantar ligaments. The other tendons do not support this foot arch.''')
add(1,'''27|How should boxes of unexposed radiographic film be positioned in storage?|Upright|Under a heavy horizontal stack|Open beside the wet bench|Directly against a heater|The storage slide specifies upright boxes in their original packaging. Heat, moisture, radiation and chemical fumes should be avoided.
27|Which records help trace film stock and identify ageing supplies?|Batch numbers and expiry dates|Only the wall paint colour|Only staff shoe sizes|Only the cassette manufacturer logo|Recording batches and expiry dates supports stock rotation and identification of expired supplies. The other details do not establish film age.
29|Which artefact appearance is associated with general film fog?|Overall grey haze|A sharply defined anatomical fracture|A normal white border only|An isolated printed patient label|Fog produces an overall grey haze and may result from unwanted light, radiation, heat, age or fumes. It is different from a localized anatomical finding.
29|Which appearance is associated with scratches in the lecture table?|Thin white lines|Uniform blackening of the entire film|A normal anatomical joint space|A printed exposure number|The handling table describes scratches as thin white lines caused by rough loading or dirty screens. They are handling damage rather than anatomy.
31|Which roll-film format is listed for mass chest screening?|100 mm|5 mm|500 mm|1 mm|The roll-film table lists 100 mm for mass chest screening. The lecture distinguishes this from the smaller cine formats.
32|Which photographic material produces positive transparencies for projection?|Slide or reversal film|Photographic paper|Used fixer|Film storage cardboard|The slide identifies reversal film as producing positive transparencies for projection. Photographic paper produces prints rather than projected transparencies.
32|Why is microfilm used for documents and images?|To reduce storage space|To increase chemical fog|To replace every X-ray detector|To prevent all patient movement|Microfilm reduces the size of recorded documents and images, saving storage space. It is a storage medium rather than an exposure-control device.
33|What does film spectral sensitivity describe?|Response to different wavelengths of light|Resistance to mechanical bending only|Thickness of the film box|Patient thickness measured in centimetres|Spectral sensitivity concerns the wavelengths or colours of light to which film responds. Matching film to screen emission helps avoid film-screen mismatch.
38|Which listed investigation uses cine film to record a dynamic study?|Coronary angiography|A static bone-density calculation only|A handwritten attendance register|A chemical stock inventory|The lecture lists coronary angiography, ventriculography and dynamic swallowing or gastrointestinal studies. Cine recording is suited to sequences showing motion.
41|Why may CT images of the same examination be printed with different window settings?|To display soft tissue lung and bone differently|To change the patient's identity|To remove the need for image acquisition|To alter the original anatomical position|The CT film slide lists separate soft-tissue, lung and bone windows. Window settings determine the displayed appearance of the image.''')
add(2,'''7|Which computer task receives digital data from an X-ray detector?|Image acquisition|Film-box storage|Chemical precipitation|Manual tank descaling|The applications slide identifies receipt of detector data as image acquisition. The other activities concern physical storage or chemical processing.
7|Which computer application adjusts image magnification?|Image processing|Patient transport|Film drying|Waste collection|Image processing includes brightness, contrast, sharpness and magnification adjustments. These are changes to image presentation.
7|Which computer function supports appointment scheduling?|Patient and examination management|Grid oscillation|Fixer hardening|Cassette scrubbing|The lecture places appointments, requests, patient records and reports under patient and examination management. These are administrative information tasks.
7|Which computer activity supports radiologists performing measurements on images?|Image diagnosis and interpretation|Silver replacement|Darkroom ventilation|Film packaging|Specialized image-viewing software supports examination and measurement of images. This is distinct from the physical processing and storage tasks in the distractors.
12|What is radiography software?|Programs used to acquire process display store and transmit images|Only the plastic computer casing|Only the power cable|Only the examination couch|Software consists of programs and applications. Physical equipment such as cables and casings is hardware.
18|Which is an example of raw examination data in the lecture?|Exposure time|An interpreted diagnostic conclusion only|A completed specialist opinion only|A final clinical recommendation only|Exposure time is a technical measurement recorded before interpretation. The other choices describe conclusions rather than raw technical data.
18|Before processing and interpretation, detector readings are classified as what?|Data|A final radiologist report|A signed diagnosis|A completed referral opinion|The lecture defines data as raw facts, observations or measurements. Detector readings become meaningful information through processing and interpretation.
23|Which data can help monitor repeat-examination practices?|Exposure and repeat-examination data|Only cafeteria sales|Only car registration numbers|Only the weather forecast|The quality-assurance section identifies exposure and repeat data as useful for monitoring imaging practices. The other records do not measure repeat imaging.
23|Why is accurate patient identification information important?|It links the correct patient to the correct examination|It replaces all image-quality checks|It determines room paint colour|It eliminates the need for communication|The lecture links patient identification to ensuring that the correct patient receives the correct examination. It does not replace other quality or communication checks.
32|Which activity belongs to digital file management?|Naming and organizing examination files|Changing the developer temperature|Moving a focused grid|Hardening film gelatin|File management includes creating, naming, organizing, storing and retrieving files. The other options are equipment or chemical-processing tasks.''')
add(3,'''10|What permits daylight film handling in normal room lighting?|An enclosed light-protected system|An uncovered film tray|Removal of all cassette seals|Direct sunlight on the emulsion|The comparison table identifies a light-tight loading system as the protection used in daylight processing. Exposed unprotected film would not be protected by normal room lighting.
10|Compared with daylight processing, darkroom processing is described as what?|Often more labour-intensive|Always completely automated|Independent of lighting control|Unable to use conventional film|The lecture comparison describes darkroom handling as often more labour-intensive. Both approaches can be used in conventional film-based radiography.
22|Which CR plate layer provides mechanical strength?|Support layer|Patient identification label|Developer layer|Fixer layer|The support layer gives the CR imaging plate mechanical strength. Developer and fixer are chemical solutions rather than CR plate layers.
22|Which CR plate layer protects the surface from damage?|Protective layer|A loose sheet of exposed film|A developer bath|A moving grid|The protective layer covers and protects the imaging plate surface. The distractors do not form its protective surface layer.
22|What releases the stored energy when a CR plate is read?|A laser in the reader|A wash-water jet|A fixer replenishment pump|A drying fan|The reader scans the imaging plate with a laser, releasing stored energy as light. The light is detected and converted into a digital image.
22|What does the photostimulable phosphor layer store after X-ray exposure?|Energy as a latent image|Liquid fixer|A paper report|A mechanical grid pattern only|The phosphor layer stores exposure energy as a latent image until readout. CR therefore separates exposure from the subsequent reader step.
28|Which sequence describes indirect-conversion DR?|X-rays to light to electrical signals|X-rays to fixer to paper|Light to developer to silver recovery|X-rays to a manual wash tank|A scintillator first converts X-rays into light, and photodiodes then convert light into electrical signals. This distinguishes indirect conversion from direct charge production.
28|Which detector type does not require a scintillator layer for its conversion process?|Direct-conversion DR|Indirect-conversion DR|A screen-film cassette|A fluorescent screen alone|Direct-conversion material converts X-rays directly into electrical charge. The indirect process instead requires an intermediate light signal.
36|Which listed material can act as a scintillator in indirect DR?|Cesium iodide|Acetic acid|Steel wool|Alum|The lecture lists cesium iodide and gadolinium oxysulfide as scintillators. The other materials belong to processing or silver-recovery contexts.
36|In indirect DR, what is the input to the photodiode array?|Light from the scintillator|Used fixer from the processor|Warm air from the dryer|A paper referral|Photodiodes convert scintillator light into electrical charge. They operate after the X-ray-to-light conversion step.''')
add(4,'''8|Which exposure factors are controlled by the X-ray generator?|kVp mA and exposure time|Only patient height and weight|Only film-box dimensions|Only room humidity and paint colour|The generator regulates the electrical supply and selected kVp, mA and time. These determine the tube operating conditions.
12|In the X-ray tube, electrons travel in which direction?|Cathode to anode|Anode to cathode|Patient to collimator|Grid to control panel|The generator sequence states that electrons travel from cathode to anode. They strike the anode target to produce X-rays and heat.
12|What is produced when the accelerated electrons strike the target?|X-rays and heat|Only visible room light|Only mechanical sound|Only wash water|The target interaction produces X-rays and heat. The electrical energy is therefore not converted solely into the useful X-ray beam.
18|Which group contains beam-limiting devices?|Aperture diaphragms cones and collimators|Fixer tanks dryers and racks|Keyboards mice and printers|Thermometers timers and sinks|These devices restrict the beam to the area of interest. The other groups serve processing, computing or darkroom functions.
18|What is the main purpose of a beam-centering device?|Indicate the direction of the central beam|Remove silver from fixer|Store a latent image|Heat the cathode filament|Centering devices indicate the central beam direction toward the area of interest. Beam limitation and chemical processing are separate functions.
18|Which is listed as a beam-centering aid?|Light beam localizer|Film hopper|Developer replenisher|Drying cabinet|The lecture lists the light beam localizer among centering devices. It aids alignment rather than processing or storing film.
22|Bremsstrahlung results from which electron interaction?|Deflection and deceleration near target atoms|Fixer dissolving silver halide|Water evaporating from film|Mechanical movement of a grid|A fast electron loses kinetic energy during deflection and deceleration, emitting an X-ray photon. This is distinct from characteristic shell-transition radiation.
22|What initiates characteristic X-ray production in the target?|Ejection of an inner-shell electron|Evaporation of wash water|Movement of the cassette tray|Opening the room door|An incident electron ejects an inner-shell target electron. An outer-shell electron fills the vacancy and the energy difference is emitted as characteristic radiation.
26|Why does the Bucky move its grid during exposure?|To blur grid lines from the image|To move the patient intentionally|To replenish fixer|To adjust patient identification|Grid movement prevents a stationary grid-line pattern from being recorded prominently. The grid itself still reduces scatter reaching the receptor.
26|Which pair of Bucky arrangements is listed in the lecture?|Vertical and horizontal table Bucky|Dry and wet developer Bucky|Cine and microfilm tank Bucky|Anode and cathode wash Bucky|The lecture identifies vertical and horizontal or table Bucky assemblies. Their orientation relates to the examination arrangement.''')
add(5,'''7|What is a reason for cleaning crossover rollers routinely?|Prevent guide-shoe marks and roller-related lines|Increase chemical contamination|Remove all image contrast|Disable film transport|The maintenance slide connects crossover-roller cleaning with preventing processing marks. Dirty transport components can transfer deposits or damage the film surface.
7|What transformation does an automatic processor perform?|Latent image to permanent visible radiograph|Visible anatomy to unexposed film|Printed report to X-ray photons|Digital file to a patient record number|The processor uses chemical processing to turn the invisible latent image into a permanent visible radiograph. It is not an X-ray generator.
11|Which processor stage immediately follows the wash tank in the listed sequence?|Dryer chamber|Feed tray|Developer tank|Fixer tank|The sequence is feed, developer, fixer, wash, dryer and receiving bin. Drying removes remaining surface moisture after washing.
11|Which component receives film after the dryer chamber?|Receiving bin|Developer reservoir|Fresh fixer bottle|Water inlet filter|The receiving bin is the final collection point in the listed transport sequence. It follows the dryer rather than a chemical tank.
11|Why must wash water remove chemical residues from processed film?|To help prevent archival degradation|To erase the permanent image|To increase retained processing chemicals|To re-expose the emulsion|Residual chemicals can cause later staining and degradation. The wash stage removes residues before drying and storage.
13|Where is a conventional darkroom feed tray located?|Inside a darkroom under suitable safelight conditions|In direct sunlight|On an open outdoor bench|Inside a silver-recovery cartridge|The lecture distinguishes the standard darkroom tray from a light-tight daylight loader. Unprotected film needs controlled lighting.
13|How can a daylight loader protect film during handling?|A light-tight chamber with sleeves or cassette unloading|A permanently open lid|A brighter ceiling lamp|A transparent unshielded tray|The enclosed loader permits manipulation while keeping room light away from the film emulsion. The alternative choices provide no light protection.
16|What triggers volume-based replenishment as described in the lecture?|Film crossing the entry sensors|The patient's surname|A change in wall colour|Opening the reporting software|Entry sensors detect film passage and trigger a dose based on film length or area. This ties replenishment to the processing workload.
23|During deep cleaning, what should be removed from the developer and fixer racks?|Gelatin build-up and chemical scale|Patient identification from the report|The tube focal spot|The image detector electronics|The weekly procedure calls for warm-water rinsing and removal of deposits from the racks. These deposits can affect transport and processing quality.
23|A drop in water pressure or flow should prompt inspection of which component?|Water supply filter|Patient wrist support|Report printer toner|Tube rotation bearing|The weekly QA slide calls for inspection and replacement of the water filter when flow or pressure drops. Adequate water supply supports washing.''')
add(6,'''22|What does the thermostatic mixer do in a manual processing unit?|Blends hot and cold water|Mixes fixer directly into developer|Moves the X-ray tube|Scans the film with a laser|The mixer blends hot and cold water to support temperature control. It does not mix the separate processing chemicals together.
22|Which component keeps tank levels safe and permits emptying?|Overflow and drain|Cassette screen|Film label|Safelight bracket|Overflow and drainage facilities control water levels and allow tanks to be emptied. Their function is separate from image formation.
28|Where should cassettes be unloaded and reloaded in the darkroom?|Dry bench|Inside the wash tank|On a wet sink edge|In the fixer bath|The dry bench is the loading and unloading area. Separating it from the wet bench helps prevent water or chemical contact with film.
29|What is the purpose of a film hopper?|Store unexposed film|Collect used fixer only|Dry wet radiographs|Move the grid|The film-storage slide identifies the hopper as a container for unexposed film. It is not a chemical-waste or drying device.
30|What is the cassette hatch used for?|Passing cassettes between darkroom and exposure room|Heating developer|Removing retained fixer|Projecting a cine sequence|The hatch is a wall link enabling cassette transfer between the rooms. Processing chemistry is handled elsewhere.
31|Which material is recommended for processing tanks in the lecture?|Polypropylene|Copper|Brass|Galvanised steel|Polypropylene and stainless steel 316 are listed as suitable tank materials. Copper, brass and galvanised steel are listed among materials to avoid.
35|What should follow scrubbing when cleaning a processing tank?|Thorough rinsing with clean water|Immediate film loading without rinsing|Adding debris to fresh developer|Sealing the dirty tank permanently|The lecture sequence is drain, scrub, rinse, descale if needed, then refill and check temperature. Rinsing removes cleaning residues.
38|What is the function of the buffer in fixer?|Maintain a steady pH|Produce the X-ray beam|Move the film hanger|Convert the image into pixels|The fixer buffer holds the pH steady. The clearing agent, acidifier and hardener perform different chemical functions.
51|Why must wet film be handled gently?|Its soft gelatin scratches easily|Its base has become a metal grid|It contains an active X-ray source|It has no emulsion remaining|The lecture warns that wet gelatin is soft and easily scratched. Gentle handling and clean filtered drying air help avoid marks.
55|Which silver-recovery method uses resin to capture silver ions?|Ion exchange|Film drying|Beam collimation|Safelight filtration|Ion-exchange resin captures silver ions. This differs from electrolytic deposition, metallic replacement and chemical precipitation.''')
add(7,'''13|What is the SI unit of work?|Joule|Ampere|Ohm|Tesla|Work is a transfer of energy and is measured in joules. The other units describe current, resistance and magnetic flux density.
13|A force of 5 N moves an object 3 m in the force direction. What work is done?|15 J|8 J|2 J|30 J|The slide gives work as force multiplied by displacement in the force direction. Thus 5 multiplied by 3 equals 15 joules.
13|Which type of energy is associated with particle motion?|Kinetic energy|Potential energy only|Atomic number|Electrical resistance|The lecture identifies kinetic energy as energy due to motion. Potential energy is associated with stored energy in a field or configuration.
17|What determines the identity of a chemical element?|Number of protons|Number of neutrons alone|Physical size of its container|Temperature of its surroundings|The proton count is the atomic number and defines the element. Changing neutron number produces isotopes of the same element.
17|Which particle has no electric charge?|Neutron|Proton|Electron|Positron|The lecture states that the neutron is neutral. Protons carry positive charge and electrons carry negative charge.
17|Atoms of one element with different neutron numbers are called what?|Isotopes|Different chemical elements necessarily|Electric currents|Resistance coils|Isotopes retain the same proton number but differ in neutron number. The element identity remains unchanged.
21|Why are the lanthanide and actinide series shown below the main periodic table?|To keep the table compact|Because they have no atomic numbers|Because they are not elements|To indicate absence of electrons|The lecture explains that these series are placed below the main table for compact layout. Their position does not remove them from the periodic system.
36|A device transfers 240 J in 4 s. What is its power?|60 W|960 W|244 W|236 W|Power equals energy divided by time. Dividing 240 joules by 4 seconds gives 60 watts.
36|Two devices transfer the same energy, but one does so in less time. Which has greater power?|The device taking less time|The device taking more time|Both must have zero power|Power cannot depend on time|Power is the rate of energy transfer. For the same energy, a shorter duration gives a larger energy-per-second value.
45|What does potential difference across a component describe?|Energy converted per unit charge|Charge created from nothing|Neutron number per atom|Mechanical mass per unit volume|The slide describes electrical energy being converted as charge passes through a component. Potential difference quantifies this energy transfer per unit charge.''')
add(8,'''5|The medial end of the clavicle is also called which end?|Sternal end|Acromial end|Olecranon end|Radial end|The clavicle has a medial sternal end and a lateral acromial end. These names reflect their articulations.
7|Which groove separates the greater and lesser humeral tubercles?|Bicipital groove|Subclavian groove|Popliteal groove|Carpal tunnel|The bicipital or intertubercular groove lies between the humeral tubercles. The other structures are in different anatomical locations.
9|Which pair belongs to the superficial extrinsic shoulder muscles?|Trapezius and latissimus dorsi|Rhomboids and levator scapulae|Subscapularis and infraspinatus|Biceps brachii and brachialis|The lecture groups trapezius and latissimus dorsi as superficial posterior thoracoappendicular muscles. Rhomboids and levator scapulae form the deep extrinsic group.
12|Which ligament bridges the intertubercular sulcus?|Transverse humeral ligament|Patellar ligament|Inguinal ligament|Sacrotuberous ligament|The transverse humeral ligament bridges the groove, forming a canal for the biceps tendon and its sheath. The other ligaments are not shoulder structures.
12|Which structure helps prevent superior displacement at the shoulder?|Coracoacromial arch|Plantar aponeurosis|Inguinal ligament|Medial meniscus|The lecture identifies the coracoacromial arch as a restraint to superior displacement. The other structures belong to the foot, groin and knee.
13|Which rotator-cuff muscle is listed for medial rotation of the shoulder?|Subscapularis|Infraspinatus|Supraspinatus|Teres minor|Subscapularis produces medial rotation. The lecture lists infraspinatus for lateral rotation and supraspinatus for initiating abduction.
17|Pectoralis minor originates from which ribs in the lecture?|Third to fifth ribs|First rib only|Tenth to twelfth ribs|All twelve ribs|The lecture gives ribs 3 to 5 as its origin and the coracoid process as its insertion. Its position is deep to pectoralis major.
18|Which nerve supplies serratus anterior?|Long thoracic nerve|Femoral nerve|Obturator nerve|Tibial nerve|The lecture gives the long thoracic nerve as the supply. Serratus anterior anchors the scapula to the thoracic cage and is associated clinically with scapular winging.
24|Which muscle forms the intermediate layer of the anterior forearm?|Flexor digitorum superficialis|Pronator quadratus|Flexor pollicis longus|Extensor indicis|The lecture identifies flexor digitorum superficialis as the sole intermediate-layer muscle. Pronator quadratus and flexor pollicis longus are deep anterior muscles.
31|Which muscle occupies the adductor compartment of the hand?|Adductor pollicis|Abductor digiti minimi|Opponens digiti minimi|Flexor carpi radialis|The adductor compartment contains adductor pollicis. The little-finger muscles belong to the hypothenar compartment, while flexor carpi radialis is in the forearm.''')
add(9,'''8|Why should members of the healthcare team understand their defined duties?|To protect patients through coordinated teamwork|To remove all professional limits|To avoid sharing relevant information|To replace every other profession|The slide states that each team member has defined duties and limits and that teamwork protects the patient. Coordination depends on understanding these roles.
14|What should a technician do when first communicating with a patient?|Greet the patient and introduce themselves|Begin exposure without speaking|Use only unexplained technical abbreviations|Ignore the patient's questions|The communication slide recommends greeting and introduction. This begins a respectful interaction before identity confirmation and explanation.
14|Which communication style is recommended for explaining an examination?|Simple non-technical language|Unexplained specialist terminology|Deliberately ambiguous wording|Silence throughout the examination|Simple language helps patients understand the explanation. The lecture also recommends active listening and allowing questions.
14|How should patient questions be handled?|Listen actively and allow questions|Discourage every question|Answer without listening|Treat all questions as refusal|The communication principles explicitly include active listening and opportunities to ask questions. This supports understanding rather than assumptions.
18|Which is listed as a patient right?|Refusal of an examination|Access to every other patient's record|Demanding alteration of another patient's report|Removing all staff responsibilities|The rights slide includes refusal, information, dignity, privacy and confidentiality. It does not grant access to other patients' confidential records.
18|Which right supports explaining the proposed examination to the patient?|Information about the procedure|Automatic disclosure of other patients' details|Guaranteed priority over all emergencies|Ownership of all departmental equipment|The patient has a right to information about the procedure. This supports clear explanation and informed participation.
18|Discussing a patient's details where unrelated people can hear conflicts with which listed right?|Privacy and confidentiality|Access to the cafeteria|Choice of room paint|Ownership of the X-ray tube|The lecture lists privacy and confidentiality among patient rights. Conversations about personal clinical information must respect those rights.
23|Which item belongs in the history obtained before an examination?|Previous imaging|Only the technician's preferred music|Only the colour of the waiting-room chairs|Only the printer serial number|The history slide includes the examination reason, previous imaging and allergies. Previous studies provide relevant examination context.
23|Which question directly addresses a listed patient-history item?|Do you have any allergies?|What is the wall paint brand?|How many office printers are present?|What is the grid manufacturer's address?|Allergies are explicitly listed in the patient-history slide. The other questions concern administrative or equipment details rather than patient history.
36|Which action helps prevent avoidable repeat exposures?|Select appropriate technique and correct exposure factors|Ignore positioning and exposure settings|Repeat every image routinely|Use the same factors for every examination|The patient-protection slide recommends appropriate technique and correct exposure factors to avoid repeats. Routine unnecessary repetition increases exposure without justification.''')

add(0,'''3|A labelled feature is identified as an epicondyle of the femur. In which region of the bone should the student look?|Distal end|Femoral head|Femoral neck|Lesser trochanter|The femur slide places the condyles and epicondyles at the distal end. The head, neck and trochanters are proximal features.
5|Which group consists entirely of tarsal bones?|Talus navicular and cuboid|Talus patella and cuboid|Navicular tibia and calcaneus|Fibula cuboid and patella|The tarsal list includes talus, navicular, cuboid, calcaneus and three cuneiforms. Patella, tibia and fibula are not tarsals.
11|Which superficial gluteal muscle assists iliopsoas and rectus femoris in thigh flexion?|Tensor fasciae latae|Quadratus femoris|Superior gemellus|Obturator internus|The slide identifies tensor fasciae latae as primarily a thigh flexor working with iliopsoas and rectus femoris. The listed deep gluteal muscles act as lateral rotators.
12|Which artery passes through the gluteal region but is described as supplying the external genitalia and perineal muscles?|Internal pudendal artery|Superior gluteal artery|Inferior gluteal artery|Lateral circumflex femoral artery|The internal pudendal artery traverses the gluteal region on its way to the perineum. The slide distinguishes its destination from the gluteal arteries' muscle supply.
16|Which pair of arteries is listed as supplying the hip joint?|Medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries|Anterior and posterior tibial arteries|Radial and ulnar arteries|Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries|The hip blood-supply slide lists medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries. It also lists the artery to the femoral head from the obturator artery.
18|Which tibial surface forms the roof of the ankle mortise and transfers weight to the talus?|Inferior surface of the distal tibia|Tibial tuberosity|Anterior tibial crest|Proximal tibial plateau|The distal tibial inferior surface forms the mortise roof. The proximal plateau articulates with the femur at the knee.
22|Which set contains all four components of quadriceps femoris?|Rectus femoris and vastus medialis lateralis and intermedius|Rectus femoris sartorius gracilis and pectineus|Biceps femoris semitendinosus semimembranosus and sartorius|Vastus medialis gracilis adductor longus and iliopsoas|The anterior-thigh slide names rectus femoris and the three vasti as quadriceps. The other sets mix muscles from different groups.
23|Which combined action best matches the posterior thigh hamstrings as a group?|Knee flexion and hip extension|Knee extension and hip flexion|Ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension|Foot eversion and knee extension|The posterior compartment slide states knee flexion and hip extension. These actions distinguish the hamstrings from the anterior knee extensors.
26|Which muscle forms the inferomedial boundary of the popliteal fossa?|Medial head of gastrocnemius|Biceps femoris|Semimembranosus|Lateral head of gastrocnemius|The medial gastrocnemius head is inferomedial. The hamstrings form the superior boundaries, and the lateral gastrocnemius head contributes inferolaterally.
28|Which bones belong to the midfoot rather than the hindfoot or forefoot?|Navicular cuboid and three cuneiforms|Talus and calcaneus only|Five metatarsals only|Fourteen phalanges only|The lecture divides the foot into hindfoot, midfoot and forefoot. Navicular, cuboid and the three cuneiforms form the midfoot.''')
add(1,'''8|Which film layer lies between the emulsion and transparent base in the listed structure?|Adhesive or subbing layer|Protective supercoat|External film wrapper|Intensifying-screen phosphor|The film-structure slide places the adhesive or subbing layer between emulsion and base. The supercoat protects the outside of the emulsion.
19|For film-screen mammography, which combination of image characteristics is emphasized?|High spatial resolution and suitable contrast|Low spatial resolution and uncontrolled fog|High processing speed regardless of detail|Large image magnification without concern for contrast|The mammography slide emphasizes high spatial resolution and suitable contrast. These are the stated design goals rather than speed at the expense of detail.
28|Fog appears during film handling despite correct chemical processing. Which darkroom check is supported by the notes?|Test the safelight and check light-tightness|Increase the fixing time without checking lighting|Replace the wash water with developer|Store opened film on the wet bench|The darkroom slide requires a light-tight room and correct safelight filter, wattage and distance. Testing lighting is relevant when fog occurs during handling.
28|Why are wet and dry working areas separated in the darkroom?|To keep film handling separate from wet processing|To expose unloaded film to room light|To allow fixer storage inside film boxes|To eliminate the need for ventilation|The slide explicitly requires separate wet and dry areas in a clean, ventilated darkroom. This separates film handling from wet processing activities.
30|What image does roll film record in the arrangement described in the notes?|An image from a screen|An image formed directly by the patient without a screen|A chemical-replenishment measurement|The result of a safelight fog test only|The roll-film slide states that it records an image from a screen rather than directly from the patient. This distinguishes the described recording arrangement.
31|Which pair of roll-film widths is listed for cine and fluoroscopic recording cameras?|16 mm and 35 mm|70 mm and 100 mm|100 mm and 105 mm|35 mm and 105 mm|The table assigns both 16 mm and 35 mm to cine and fluoroscopic recording cameras. The larger listed formats have other stated uses.
39|An existing radiograph is needed for a second opinion without sending the only original. Which material fits the notes?|Duplicating film|Unexposed intraoral film|A photostimulable phosphor plate used as a document copier|Fresh developer alone|The duplicating-film slide lists referral for a second opinion, teaching, medico-legal use and backup. It is intended to copy an existing radiograph.
40|Where are images prepared before printing an MRI hard-copy sheet?|At the workstation|Inside a manual developer tank|At a film hopper|At the safelight filter|The MRI slide states that images are produced on the workstation and printed from there. The film is a hard-copy output rather than the acquisition process.
40|Which set of sequences can appear together in the MRI multi-sequence layout described?|Proton density T1 T2 and FLAIR|kVp mA mAs and exposure time|Developer fixer wash and dry|Scaphoid lunate triquetrum and pisiform|The MRI hard-copy slide lists proton density, T1, T2 and FLAIR on one sheet. These are sequence labels, unlike exposure factors, processing stages or bone names.
40|Which pair matches the typical MRI image matrices listed in the notes?|256 × 256 and 512 × 512 pixels|16 × 16 and 35 × 35 pixels|70 × 100 and 100 × 105 pixels|1 × 2 and 2 × 4 pixels|The MRI slide lists 256 by 256 and 512 by 512 pixels. These values describe image matrices rather than physical film widths.''')
add(2,'''20|A detector value is processed into a meaningful displayed image. Which transition has occurred?|Data to information|Information to computer hardware|Software to electricity|A report to raw detector charge|The comparison table defines data as raw facts and information as processed meaningful data. A displayed image is an information output in the lecture workflow.
22|Which order correctly represents the information workflow in the notes?|Collection processing output interpretation|Interpretation output collection processing|Output collection interpretation processing|Processing interpretation collection output|The workflow slide gives data collection, processing, information output, then interpretation and decision-making. The radiologist's report follows interpretation.
26|Which departmental task is a stated use of word processing?|Preparing equipment-maintenance forms|Reconstructing CT detector signals|Reading out a flat-panel detector|Regulating X-ray tube voltage|Word processing can create checklists, QA forms and maintenance records. Detector processing and generator control require different software and systems.
30|A copied report contains the previous patient's details. Which stated disadvantage of word processing does this illustrate?|Errors introduced by incorrect typing or copying|Failure of the image detector to absorb X-rays|Loss of grid movement during exposure|Exhaustion of processing chemicals|The slide explicitly warns that incorrect typing or copying can introduce errors. Document preparation therefore still needs careful checking.
30|A report is lost after a computer failure because no saved copy exists. Which limitation in the notes applies?|Data loss when documents are not saved or backed up|Insufficient film-screen contact|Excessive developer temperature|Incorrect safelight wavelength|The word-processing disadvantages include data loss without saving or backup. This is a document-storage failure rather than a film-processing problem.
36|How can well-managed image files help reduce unnecessary repeat examinations?|Make suitable previous images available for comparison|Guarantee every old image is suitable for every new request|Replace the need to identify the patient|Automatically correct every acquisition error|The slide qualifies that previous images must be available and suitable. File management supports access and comparison, without guaranteeing that no further imaging is needed.
38|Which digital method is listed for virtual consultations and educational meetings?|Video conferencing|A film hopper|A cassette hatch|A developer control strip|The communication slide lists video conferencing for virtual meetings, consultations and education. The other items are physical film-handling or QA tools.
38|Which system is listed for managing examination requests and reporting workflows?|RIS|A word processor alone|A film densitometer|A daylight loader|The methods slide assigns requests, patient details and reporting workflows to RIS. PACS is separately described for sharing, storing and retrieving images.
40|Image transmission is delayed while the workstation itself still functions. Which listed disadvantage best fits?|Poor internet connectivity|An increase in film-base thickness|A reduction in fixer acidity|A change in cassette size|The digital-communication slide states that poor connectivity can delay transmission. A working workstation does not guarantee a working network connection.
43|A student needs published clinical studies for an assignment. Which internet-research activity in the notes is most relevant?|Accessing scientific journals|Changing exposure factors on the console|Cleaning processor racks|Selecting a film hopper size|The research slide identifies access to scientific journals as a way to find published studies and evidence-based information. The other tasks do not retrieve research literature.''')
add(3,'''15|Which digital-processing adjustment is specifically listed alongside brightness contrast and sharpness?|Noise adjustment|Developer replenishment|Fixer hardening|Film drying|The slide lists brightness, contrast, sharpness and noise as image characteristics adjusted by processing algorithms. Chemical stages are not digital image adjustments.
15|Where is the processed digital radiograph presented for viewing in the stated workflow?|Diagnostic monitor|Developer tank|Unexposed film hopper|Fixer cartridge|The image-display stage uses a diagnostic monitor. Storage and transmission follow as separate functions in the workflow.
15|Which system is named for storing and electronically transmitting processed images?|PACS|A safelight housing|A thermostatic mixer|A film hanger rack|The slide names Picture Archiving and Communication System for image storage and transmission. The other devices belong to film-processing facilities.
17|During CR exposure, where is the cassette containing the imaging plate positioned?|Behind the patient in the transmitted beam|Inside the X-ray tube housing|Between the cathode and anode|Inside a wash-water tank|The exposure slide places the cassette behind the patient. The transmitted X-rays then strike the phosphor layer of the imaging plate.
22|Which CR plate layer helps control light collection depending on plate design?|Light-reflecting or absorbing layer|Fixer hardener layer|Cathode filament|Grid drive motor|The component slide assigns control of light collection to the reflecting or absorbing layer. The support layer instead provides mechanical strength.
30|Why do different regions of the DR detector receive different transmitted X-ray intensities?|Different tissues absorb different amounts of radiation|Every tissue absorbs exactly the same amount|The detector replaces the patient during exposure|The wash tank selects the transmitted beam|The DR exposure slide explains that tissue absorption differs. The remaining X-rays reach the detector and carry this spatial information.
37|Which component collects and measures signals from detector pixels before digital conversion?|Readout electronics|Protective housing alone|Scintillator cover only|A CR cassette transport handle|The readout electronics collect and measure pixel signals and send them for conversion into digital data. Protective components have a mechanical role.
37|A detector produces analog electrical measurements. Which component converts them into numerical values?|Analog-to-digital converter|Protective support layer|Film squeegee roller|Beam-centering lamp|The ADC converts measured analog electrical signals into numerical values the computer can process. It is distinct from signal collection and physical protection.
37|What is the role of the DR protective housing and support layers?|Protect and mechanically support sensitive components|Convert analog signals into numbers|Select the patient's exposure factors|Erase the stored CR image|The slide gives the housing and support layers a protective and mechanical function. Conversion and image processing are performed by other components.
40|Which task is eliminated in the usual DR workflow described compared with CR?|Routine cassette transport to a separate CR reader|Patient identification before exposure|Image review after exposure|Electronic image storage|The DR advantages slide states that routine cassette transport to a separate CR reader is eliminated. Patient identification, review and storage remain part of imaging practice.''')
add(4,'''7|Which component mainly supports patient positioning and image-detector placement?|X-ray table and wall stand|Exposure timer|Filament transformer|Rectifier|The equipment slide assigns patient positioning and detector placement to the table and wall stand. The other components regulate the electrical exposure process.
11|A generator must raise the incoming voltage to accelerate electrons across the tube. Which component performs this function?|High-tension transformer|Filament transformer|Exposure timer|Control-console display|The high-tension transformer increases voltage across the tube. The filament transformer instead provides low voltage and high current for filament heating.
11|Which electrical output is supplied by the filament transformer?|Low voltage and high current|High voltage and negligible current|No voltage and no current|Alternating high tension directly to the target|The slide specifies low voltage with high current to heat the filament and cause thermionic emission. This differs from the high-voltage tube supply.
11|Which component makes tube current unidirectional from an AC supply?|Rectifier|Exposure timer|Collimator|Cassette tray|The rectifier converts AC into unidirectional current for the X-ray tube. Beam limitation and exposure timing are separate functions.
11|Which generator component stores electrical energy and can smooth voltage fluctuations in some designs?|Capacitor|Grid strip|Focal spot|Film hanger|The slide lists capacitors for storing electrical energy and helping smooth fluctuations in certain generator designs. This is distinct from voltage transformation.
14|At fixed exposure time, increasing mA directly increases which tube quantity?|Rate of electron flow|Duration of exposure|Distance from source to image|Width of the collimated field|The factor table defines mA as controlling the rate of electron flow and affecting X-ray quantity. It does not set time, distance or field width.
14|A technique uses 200 mA for 0.1 s. What is the tube current-time product?|20 mAs|2000 mAs|200.1 mAs|0.0005 mAs|The slide defines mAs as mA multiplied by exposure time. Therefore 200 multiplied by 0.1 equals 20 mAs.
14|Which change most directly increases electron energy and strongly affects beam penetration?|Increasing kVp|Increasing exposure time alone|Increasing the number of images filed|Increasing the cassette-storage space|The exposure-factor table assigns electron energy and beam penetration primarily to kVp. Time and mA principally affect X-ray quantity.
20|An abnormal power supply causes a protective device to discontinue the circuit. Which device matches the description?|Circuit breaker|Beam alignment indicator|Image receptor tray|Exposure chart|The circuitry slide describes the circuit breaker as interrupting the circuit during an abnormal supply. The other items do not perform this electrical protection function.
26|Which Bucky component holds the image receptor?|Detector holding tray|Grid drive mechanism|High-voltage transformer|Filament transformer|The Bucky assembly list includes a detector holding tray. Its role differs from the mechanisms that move the grid or generate tube power.''')
add(5,'''9|How does roller contact assist chemical action during automatic processing?|Brings fresh chemicals to the emulsion while displacing exhausted solution|Prevents any chemical contact with the emulsion|Replaces fixing with dry heating|Erases the latent image before development|The slide describes continuous mechanical agitation as bringing fresh chemicals to the surface while squeezing out exhausted solution. This supports more uniform processing.
9|Which transport feature helps standardize the time film spends in the processor?|A fixed-speed roller path through tanks and dryer|A random manual pull speed|Different film routes chosen during each exposure|Stopping film indefinitely in developer|The transport system pulls film through a defined serpentine path at fixed speed. A repeatable path and speed support consistent processing time.
12|Besides starting the drive motor and dryer, what can the feed-tray detection signal determine?|Required replenisher volume|Patient diagnosis|X-ray photon energy|Anatomical side marker position|The feed mechanism detects film presence, size and orientation and helps calculate replenishment. It does not interpret the patient's image or set the acquired photon energy.
14|Which rollers guide film downward and upward inside the chemical tanks?|Transport or deep-tank rollers|Entrance rollers only|Squeegee rollers only|Receiving-bin rollers only|The deep-tank roller racks guide film through the liquids. Entrance rollers grip the incoming film and squeegee rollers remove water near the dryer.
15|Where are crossover rollers located relative to adjacent processing tanks?|Above solution level between tanks|Only at the bottom of the developer|Inside the receiving bin after drying|Inside the fresh replenisher bottle|The slide places crossover rollers above solution levels between adjacent tanks. Their guide shoes redirect the film into the next section.
15|Why does removing excess water with squeegee rollers help the dryer?|It reduces the thermal drying load|It increases the amount of water to evaporate|It replaces washing with fixation|It makes the film photosensitive again|Stripping excess water before the dryer leaves less water to evaporate. The lecture explicitly links this to reduced drying-energy demand.
17|Why is flood replenishment useful even when no films enter a low-volume processor?|It counters chemical deterioration from aerial oxidation|It develops a new latent image without exposure|It eliminates the need for fixer|It converts wash water into replenisher|The slide describes timed pulses of fresh chemistry regardless of film throughput to combat aerial oxidation. Replenishment is therefore not always workload-triggered.
20|Which shutdown action in the notes helps prevent water waste or flooding?|Turn off the main water valve|Leave the water supply running at full flow|Block the drain while leaving water on|Fill the feed tray with water|The shutdown routine explicitly calls for turning off the main water valve. This controls water supply when the processor is no longer operating.
20|Why is the main processor lid left slightly open overnight in the described shutdown routine?|To vent residual humidity|To expose patient films to white light|To increase condensation on the cover|To keep fresh film in the chemical tanks|The slide recommends slightly propping the lid open to vent residual humidity. It also discusses reducing condensation and chemical-crystal dripping.
22|Which group of control-film measurements is listed for daily processor QA?|Base plus fog speed index and contrast index|Pulse respiratory rate and blood pressure|kVp source distance and patient height|Film width cassette weight and room area|The daily QA slide lists base plus fog, speed index and contrast index measured from a processed control film. These assess processing stability.''')
add(6,'''7|In a monobath workflow, which stages are still required after development and fixation?|Washing and drying|Only another X-ray exposure|Only cassette reloading while wet|No further treatment|The monobath sequence still includes washing residual chemicals away and hanging film to dry. Combining chemical actions does not remove these later stages.
10|Which finding is a listed limitation of single-bath processing?|Rapid result drift because bath life is short|Unlimited bath life|Independent developer and fixer control|Complete immunity to temperature changes|The single-bath slide lists short bath life and quickly drifting results. It contrasts with separate solutions that can be controlled independently.
14|Why do separate developer and fixer baths permit better chemical control?|Each can be replenished and replaced on its own schedule|Both must always be discarded after each film|The two solutions must have identical pH|Separate baths eliminate all operator timing|The slide emphasizes independent replenishment and replacement. Developer and fixer have different chemical roles and are alkaline and acidic respectively.
19|Which is a stated limitation of conventional manual processing?|Dependence on operator skill|Requirement for a CR laser reader|Inability to replenish solutions|Absence of running-water needs|The manual-processing slide lists slow, labour-intensive operation and reliance on operator skill. Replenishment is listed as an advantage, not a limitation.
21|What defines the integrated manual processing system in the notes?|Processing tanks combined in one ordered unit|Every chemical mixed in one solution|A detector panel without chemical tanks|A separate room for each tank|The integrated system combines developer, rinse, fixer and wash tanks in processing order. It keeps separate stages within one temperature environment.
24|Which trade-off applies to the integrated manual processing unit?|Stable temperature but low throughput|No temperature control but unlimited throughput|No water requirement but compulsory CR scanning|High throughput with no operator involvement|The slide lists stable solution temperature as an advantage and low throughput and labour intensity as limitations. These distinguish it from automated high-throughput operation.
34|Which task is placed in the weekly maintenance schedule?|Clean hangers and rods and check the thermometer|Replace every solution after each film|Perform no checks until an artefact appears|Remove the wet bench permanently|The weekly schedule includes hangers, rods, tank rims, safelight and thermometer checks. The lecture separates these from daily and monthly tasks.
36|Which set contains the four chemical-processing variables to control?|Time temperature concentration and purity|kVp mA distance and field size|Pulse temperature respiration and blood pressure|Film width report length room size and staffing|The control-of-chemistry slide names time, temperature, concentration and purity. These affect processing consistency and repeat risk.
41|A crescent-shaped handling mark appears after film has been kinked. Which preventive action matches the notes?|Hold by the edges and avoid flexing|Bend the film more tightly|Increase the safelight wattage|Shorten washing without inspection|The handling-artefact table links crescent marks to bending or kinking. Edge handling without flexing addresses that mechanical cause.
47|Why is the hanger tapped at the start of manual development?|To dislodge air bells from the film|To increase fixer carry-over|To scratch the wet emulsion|To expose the film to white light|The development-stage slide instructs tapping the hanger to remove air bells. Agitation and removal of trapped bubbles help chemical contact with the film.''')
add(7,'''6|Which conversion expresses one electron-volt in joules as given in the table?|1.6022 × 10⁻¹⁹ J|1.6022 × 10¹⁹ J|1.6605 × 10⁻²⁷ J|1 × 10⁻¹⁰ J|The conversion table gives one electron-volt as 1.6022 times ten to the minus nineteen joules. The atomic mass and length conversions have different quantities and units.
6|Which unit is equivalent to 10⁻¹⁰ metre in the notes?|Angstrom|Fermi|Barn|Coulomb|The table gives one angstrom as ten to the minus ten metres and one fermi as ten to the minus fifteen metres. A barn measures area rather than length.
16|Where is more than 99.9 percent of atomic mass concentrated according to the notes?|Nucleus|Outer electron cloud|Space between atoms|Valence shell alone|The atomic-structure slide describes a tiny dense nucleus containing almost all the atom's mass. The surrounding electron region accounts for most of its volume.
20|What are the horizontal rows of the periodic table called?|Periods|Groups|Isotopes|Nucleons|The periodic-table slide identifies periods as horizontal rows and groups as vertical columns. These terms describe the table's organization.
20|Why do elements within a group have similar chemical properties in the explanation given?|They have the same number of valence electrons|They necessarily have identical mass numbers|They all contain no neutrons|They all have identical total proton counts|The slide links group similarity to valence electrons. Identical proton counts would identify the same element rather than a group of different elements.
39|At constant current and resistance, how does doubling the duration affect Joule heating?|Doubles the total heat|Halves the total heat|Quadruples the total heat|Leaves total heat unchanged|The lecture identifies longer duration as increasing total heat. With current and resistance fixed, heat is proportional to time, so doubling time doubles heat.
39|Why is nichrome used in the heating appliances listed?|Its high resistance helps convert electrical energy into heat|It prevents any electrical energy conversion|It has no electrical resistance|It converts heat directly into X-ray photons|The heating slide contrasts high-resistance nichrome with lower-resistance copper and lists heaters and kettles as applications. Resistance allows electrical heating.
41|Which process supplies heat in the nuclear electricity source described?|Nuclear fission|Chemical film fixation|Laser stimulation of phosphor|Electron-shell excitation alone|The electrical-energy slide describes splitting atomic nuclei to provide heat for steam and turbines. That process is nuclear fission.
43|Which electrical output is associated with voltaic cells in the notes?|Direct current|A continuous X-ray spectrum|An acoustic pressure wave only|A magnetic field with no electric current|The slide states that voltaic cells and electrochemical reactions produce DC electricity. This is an electrical-source description rather than an X-ray generation process.
49|Which X-ray effect means some crystals emit visible light after irradiation?|Fluorescent effect|Thermionic emission|Nuclear fission|Electrical rectification|The properties slide identifies fluorescence as visible-light emission from certain crystals under X-rays. Thermionic emission is electron release from a heated filament.''')
add(8,'''4|Which sequence correctly orders the main parts of the brachial plexus?|Roots trunks divisions cords branches|Roots cords trunks branches divisions|Branches divisions roots cords trunks|Trunks roots branches divisions cords|The brachial-plexus slide lists roots, trunks, divisions, cords and branches in sequence. The other options rearrange those stages.
5|The lateral end of the clavicle is named for which articulation?|Acromial end|Sternal end|Trochlear end|Capitular end|The clavicle slide names its ends sternal medially and acromial laterally. Trochlea and capitulum are distal humeral structures.
7|Which projections are located at the distal humerus?|Medial and lateral epicondyles|Greater and lesser trochanters|Medial and lateral malleoli|Medial and lateral cuneiforms|The humerus slide lists medial and lateral epicondyles at the distal end. The other pairs belong to lower-limb anatomy.
9|Which pair belongs to the deep extrinsic shoulder group?|Levator scapulae and rhomboids|Trapezius and latissimus dorsi|Deltoid and teres major|Subscapularis and infraspinatus|The lecture divides extrinsic shoulder muscles into superficial and deep groups. Levator scapulae and rhomboids are named as the deep group.
12|Which tendon passes through the capsular opening between the greater and lesser humeral tubercles?|Long head of biceps brachii|Long head of triceps brachii|Distal tendon of brachialis|Common extensor tendon|The shoulder-support slide describes passage of the long-head biceps tendon between the tubercles. This relates to the intertubercular groove and transverse humeral ligament.
13|Which muscle is specifically listed for lateral rotation of the shoulder?|Infraspinatus|Subscapularis|Pectoralis major|Anterior deltoid|The movement slide lists infraspinatus for external rotation and subscapularis for medial rotation. The other options do not match the stated lateral-rotation entry.
18|Which attachment is given for subclavius on the clavicle?|Subclavian groove on the inferior middle third|Acromial tip only|Superior surface of the lateral epicondyle|Radial tuberosity|The pectoral-region slide places subclavius on the inferior clavicular subclavian groove. The radial tuberosity and epicondyle are not clavicular sites.
24|Which group contains the three deep anterior forearm muscles listed?|Flexor digitorum profundus flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus|Pronator teres palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis|Flexor digitorum superficialis brachialis and anconeus|Extensor indicis extensor digitorum and supinator|The anterior-compartment slide lists FDP, FPL and pronator quadratus as the deep layer. FDS is intermediate and the common superficial flexors are separate.
26|Which muscle is listed among the posterior forearm extensors of the medial four digits?|Extensor indicis|Flexor pollicis longus|Flexor carpi ulnaris|Pronator teres|The posterior-compartment slide places extensor indicis in its group of medial-four-digit extensors. The other choices belong to the anterior forearm flexor-pronator group.
32|Which muscles are listed within the central midpalmar compartment?|Lumbricals|Deltoid fibres|Rhomboids|Intercostals|The central-compartment slide includes lumbricals with long flexor tendons, arterial arches and portions of median and ulnar nerves. The distractor muscles are outside the palm.''')
add(9,'''9|Before producing an image, which paired preparation is identified as part of the technician's role?|Prepare the patient and equipment|Prepare only the report after exposure|Prepare only the waiting-room register|Prepare only the film archive after reporting|The role slide begins with preparation of both patient and equipment. It also emphasizes safe image production and radiation protection.
15|A patient appears distressed without saying so. Which communication action matches the notes?|Observe the patient's facial expressions|Rely only on the request form|Ignore non-verbal signs|Continue without acknowledging the patient|The non-verbal communication slide asks staff to watch facial expressions as well as use a calm tone and open posture. Communication includes more than spoken answers.
15|Which condition should guide physical touch during communication?|Permission and a clear purpose|Routine touch without explanation|Touch as a substitute for consent|Touch whenever questions are inconvenient|The slide explicitly says to touch only with permission and purpose. This supports respectful non-verbal communication.
16|A patient cannot follow instructions because the room is noisy and others can overhear. Which listed barrier applies?|Noise and lack of privacy|Insufficient processing temperature|Incorrect image matrix|Poor screen-film contact|The communication-barriers slide includes noise and lack of privacy. These can hinder communication even when equipment is functioning.
16|Which pair of patient factors is listed as a communication barrier?|Pain and fear|Image brightness and contrast|Film size and base tint|Tube voltage and current|The slide identifies pain, fear and anxiety among communication barriers. The other options are image or equipment variables.
22|Which details are explicitly checked against the examination request during preparation?|Name age sex and examination|Only the patient's first name|Only the date of the last equipment service|Only the room number|The assessment slide requires checking the request completely and verifying name, age, sex and examination. A single detail alone does not fulfil that list.
29|Which pair of patient-transfer methods is listed?|Wheelchair and trolley|Only walking without assistance|Only carrying by one staff member|Only moving the imaging detector|The transfer slide names wheelchair and trolley as the principal methods. The question asks for the methods in the supplied notes rather than a transfer protocol.
30|Which approach to a paediatric patient is recommended in the notes?|Friendly playful interaction with parent or guardian involvement|Use unexplained technical language and exclude the guardian|Ignore the child's fear and rush instructions|Avoid all communication until after the exposure|The paediatric-care slide recommends a friendly, playful approach and involvement of a parent or guardian. This is a communication and cooperation principle.
43|How does clear patient flow support departmental management?|Reduces waiting time|Eliminates the need for patient identification|Replaces all reporting duties|Allows records to be discarded after exposure|The organisation slide links clear patient flow to shorter waiting and effective health management. It does not remove clinical or record-keeping responsibilities.
44|Which statement reflects the teamwork principle in the notes?|Each member has specific duties and deserves respect|Only one profession has defined duties|Respect is needed only during an emergency|Professional development is unrelated to teamwork|The collaboration slide emphasizes specific duties, respect for every team member and continuing professional development. This supports coordinated care.''')

